const academicPapers = [{"id": "paper-one", "sourceTitle": "Revised Synthesis Paper", "text": "Kantheti \r\nNalini Kantheti\r\nENGW 1111\r\nJeremy Bushnell\r\n04/15/2022\r\nIndian Beauty Standards and British Colonization\r\n        How bizarre is it that a community composed almost entirely of medium to dark skinned people is obsessed with having fair skin? By ‘obsessed’, I mean having a four billion dollar skin bleaching industry with its most popular product called Fair & Lovely (Reuters). It seems ridiculous, right? In India, this preference to have lighter skin has been embedded in its culture for centuries. Now, the modern beauty standard not only demands lighter skin, but also a slender figure, slimmer nose, lighter eyes, and commends having Eurocentric features in general. I wonder why. It seems we can still see the lasting legacy of colonization reflected in today’s India, more than a half-century after achieving independence, in the most obvious form of persisting colorism.\r\nBut that’s just one way in which colonization forever changed India’s internal beauty standards, and it’s more informative to examine the bigger picture. How were South Asian beauty standards impacted by British colonization? There are several complex ways in which British and Indian cultures reacted to each other, but in short, British colonization impacted Indian beauty standards by further oppressing women, promoting Eurocentric ideals, and heavily reinforcing colorism.\r\n        However, analyzing these cultural changes in context with the culture as a whole can paint a clearer picture. Before British colonization, the Indian beauty standard largely followed the natural appearance of its adherents, with a few alterations. The first depictions of women seem to appear in the form of Mauryan figures around the first to fourth century BCE. They “presented full breasts, wide hips and tapered legs”, which fit in with other ancient beauty standards as they were often centered around fertility. However, around the first century BCE, Sunga figurines “contorted the body into S-shaped curves to emphasize sexuality”, and by the Kushan era, these figurines began to feature “eyes that were placed two-thirds of the way up the face… breast[s that] were places one heads height below the chin… and the use of a fairly round face.” The particularity of these proportions can indicate an idealized beauty standard, and that these were not the natural proportions of Indian women (Chen 4).  \r\n  \r\n          \r\n         Mauryan Figure (Majidi)        Sunga Figure (Majidi)           Kushan Figure (Majidi)\r\n\r\n\r\nAnother representation of the Indian beauty ideal stems from the portrayal of Hindu goddesses, especially Parvati, the goddess of love, devotion and fertility, who is characterized as “the grand personification of … the concept of beauty”. She is described to be “a slender-bodied maiden of comely hips and [a] moon-like face” that is often pale or glowing. Moreover, her facial features are described to have “eyes as lotus petals, .. [a] lower lip like the Bimba, … and her nose like the beak of a parrot” (Majidi). From these descriptions, it's clear that fertility was an influential and recurring theme in ancient Indian beauty ideals, with the most desirable qualities being wide hips and a round face. \r\nThis beauty ideal did not change much throughout the later centuries, as medium complexioned skin color, thick black hair, and a curvy figure remained as preferable qualities to have in India. This is evidenced by a marriage advertisement for a thirteen year old girl in Calcutta in 1910, in which her family describes her as having a “medium complexion skin color, and good figure.” Another girl’s parents in 1927 wrote that their daughter has an “Ujjwala Shyama varna,” or a ‘glistening dark complexion’. Additionally, a Bengali writer, Mohitlal Majumdar (1888 - 1952), characterized the ideal Bengali woman as “medium complexioned, with wide downcast eyes; [with] tresses [that] are black, wavy, and flowing… she shows no dearth of modesty and decency” (Majidi). \r\nFrom all of these descriptions, we can draw a general and traditional beauty standard that features large eyes, a narrow waist but wider hips and breasts, and long black hair. It’s important to note that though having medium toned or golden skin was most desirable, having dark skin was not necessarily unattractive at the time. For centuries, the beauty standard in India was mostly unchanging and followed some cyclical alterations. It is also more closely aligned with the average Indian woman’s appearrance, making it more realistic and attainable. \r\nEverything changed when the British colonized India. Not only did the beauty standard drastically shift, but the British also affected the fundamental role of women in society. Though the new laws and policies had a significant impact on the status of all women, it varied based on class and caste. Poor women suffered a dual loss, as the destruction of the Indian system of industry and agriculture eroded their economic position. Additionally, all women lost their social freedoms “as they came under the restrictive Hindu norms which were now universally applied by the British.” In 1722, the governor-general of Bengal, William Hastings, “declared Hindu texts as the sole source of legal authority, thus imposing upper caste Hindu norms on lower caste women” (Thomas).  This meant that Indian women were now expected to follow the social and moral constraints of those laws. However, it’s important to note that the British only used Hindu laws in personal areas such as marriage, divorce, and inheritance. British parliamentary law came first in all other areas. Evidently, the British wanted to further suppress Indian women by ruthlessly removing their freedoms, most likely to further legitimize their rule.\r\nNot only did these new reforms affect the lives of women, but they restructured entire families as well. For example, “the Nayars of Malabar in Kerala maintained a matrilineal form of family structure until the British imposed legal restrictions in the 19th century. A Nayar family consisted of a woman, her sisters and brothers and her children…  Neither partner received any rights or duties towards the other, however the woman received the right to sexual activity.” Unfortunately, the British introduced a number of legal changes that undermined the Nayar’s family structure by completely changing the role of the father: “In 1868 the British passed a law that a man had to provide for his wife and children… The Nayar father thus gained the right to the property of his wife's family” (Thomas). The British position regarding women’s status is certainly not favorable, but this seems to be less intentional and more of a disregard for Indian women’s well being in general. Women of all castes were affected by their reforms to different degrees, but many suffered the loss of their economic and sexual rights. This set the stage for a new era of India, and with it came a new beauty standard to follow.\r\nAs we’ve already established, the ideal Indian beauty standard was medium complexioned skin, a narrow waist but wider hips, round breasts, thick black hair, and a moon-like face. The modern beauty standard looks a little different. As mentioned before, it heavily draws on European features, “such as their light skin and light colored eyes.” Consequently, “Fair and beautiful are synonymous” (Chen). The new ideal demands lighter skin from women with a historically darker complexion. But that’s not the only thing that changed. “Historical images of Indian women revealed the preference of curvy hourglass figures and even some extra fat in the stomach area, [but] the modern ideal is dominated by slim figures” (Majidi). The standard morphed to better suit lighter skinned and slim figured women, neither of which are known to be traditional Indian features. It’s more reminiscent of a European woman than an Indian one, which is highly troubling but fairly obvious as to why. \r\nEven the clothing that was worn did not escape the ramifications of British rule. The saree, traditionally worn as one single piece of fabric (except upper class women who wore a breast band called a ‘Pratidhi’) was permanently modified, so that a petticoat or blouse was now needed, presumably for modesty. Who else could have caused this change? “The culture of wearing blouses came from the British.” (The Effect). This alteration is still present in modern day sarees, and it is often assumed that blouses and petticoats were always a part of the outfit, despite the English name. This is so ingrained in Indian culture that its origin is rarely questioned. \r\nMost disturbingly however, the existing colorism in India thrived under British rule, and grew into an unquenchable monster of an issue that has plagued Indians for decades. “A legacy said to have been left by Western imperialism is having fair skin” (Chen). The magnitude of this preference for fair skin can be demonstrated by examining a popular beauty product known as Fair & Lovely. “The fairness cream brand ‘Fair and Lovely’ ha[s] been notorious for perpetuating archaic beauty standards that fairer women achieve more success than darker women” (Chen). This is an extremely popular skin bleaching cream that has dominated the Indian beauty market for decades. Many of their advertisements feature a ‘dark’ skinned woman whose problems are fixed by a lighter skinned woman graciously gifting her some Fair & Lovely cream. I remember watching their ads myself at a young age and believing that my superficial problems were due to the darkness of my skin. As NatureDreamGirl on Twitter puts it, “dark skinned women turning to steroids, drugs, and other methods in order to be considered pretty is heartbreaking and unacceptable.” \r\nShe and I are not alone in reprimanding this phenomenon. Ashwinder Suden on Twitter also acknowledges the harmful effects of colorism on India’s youngest generation: “Imagine being a dark skinned girl in India. You’d be bombarded with the media portraying women with lighter skin as the peak of beauty. You’d be shown ads for skin bleaching your entire life. You’d be called ‘ugly’ because of your dark skin at some point… Colourism and Eurocentric beauty standards have robbed billions of people of their ability to feel confident and have created an industry willing to prey on those manufactured insecurities.” This is the reality for virtually all dark skinned Indian women, after colorism took root during British rule and seeped into every aspect of life since: beauty, status, and self worth. If you are dark skinned, you are automatically at a disadvantage through no fault of your own.\r\nKhesraw Majidi, an accomplished Social Sciences masters student at Humboldt University shares an anecdote in his article that further illustrates how disturbing the importance of skin tone in Indian beauty is. While traveling on a train in India, he saw a magazine with some articles written by schoolchildren, in which an eleven-year-old girl writes: “I am in 5th class… I like my history teacher because she is ‘Gori’, (white or fair) she is gorgeous, and a kind woman… And I do not like my math’s teacher because she is ‘Kali’ (black or darker color) and she is ugly and bad”. Think about how you would react if you happened to come across this article. Most Americans will scoff at such blatant colorism, but for people of color, this comes as no surprise. Colorism is so deeply rooted in Indian culture that not only is this kind of thinking not abnormal, but commonplace. \r\nAward winning writer, artist, and activist Zahira Kelley explores the lasting consequences of colonialism in her lecture at Boston College. “She argued that society’s understanding of beauty was profoundly altered by colonization and imperialism, which imposed a standard of ‘whiteness’ to measure beauty. This, she continued, led to the notion that ‘whiter is better’” (Fenoglio). Though not of Indian origin, she also discusses colorism in colored societies. “The beauty standards imposed on minorities are also caused by a ‘hierarchy’ within people of color… Specifically, colorism, which represents discrimination based on one’s skin tone, establishes these hierarchies within race. Those who most closely resemble Europeans rise to the top of the caste system” (Fenoglio). This notion is very present in Indian societies, as those with European features are considered to be the most beautiful and good, evidenced by the little girl’s words from the magazine. This notion that black or white somehow correlates to inherent goodness or badness is not a mistake and is in fact a very intentional byproduct of colonialism. \r\nHowever, to truly understand the idea that ‘white is good’ and ‘fair is beautiful’ in India, we must take a step back and observe postcolonial societies as a whole. Celebrated French Indian philosopher and psychiatrist Frantz Fanon explores this idea in his book, Black Skin, White Masks. In his book, Fanon argues that not only does colonization reap territorial and economic benefits, but also leaves a profound psychological influence on the colonized society. Fanon describes colonized people as those “in whose soul an inferiority complex has been created by the death and burial of its local cultural originality” (Fanon 18). Majidi agrees with and expands upon this idea, affirming that “people of color adopt the same values and ideals as the invaders and create a vision that deviates them from their world because they understand they will never and cannot become a white nation.” Colonialism breeds a mindset of inferiority in the colonized people, while those who most closely resemble the colonizers are treated better and rise to the top. In the case of India, “British colonialists promoted this notion by attributing Indians with fairer skin to the higher class and caste, of course while remaining inferior to the colonizers, and the darker Indians to the lower class and caste, ugly and cheap people” (Majidi). \r\nBritish colonization changed Indian beauty standards in such a drastic way that it still holds influence over them a half century after Indian independence. By further subjugating the status of women, imprinting Eurocentric standards onto the colonized population, and heavily strengthening the existing colorism in India, the British have forever changed what it means to be an Indian woman. Consider your culture's ideal beauty standard. Imagine how absurd it would be if you found that most of the things that seemed to be ingrained in your culture were actually the product of an outsider's influence. This is the pill the modern Indian woman must swallow. She knows that she must bleach her skin, wear blouses with her sarees, and maintain a slender figure to be beautiful, even though none of these things ever came naturally to her. \r\n        The most troubling aspect of this issue is that it’s not limited to India. Consider the island of Fiji, whose beauty standards remained the same until just recently, when televisions were introduced to the island. In previous years, “[a] robust, nicely rounded body [was] the norm for men and women. ‘Skinny legs’ [was] a major insult. And ‘going thin’... [was] considered a worrisome condition”.  However, after televisions became widely available in 1995 and young women were subjected to Western media, the beauty ideal drastically shifted. “Just a few years after the introduction of television… eating disorders are on the rise among young girls, according to a study presented at the American Psychiatric Association meetings in Washington.  Young girls dream of looking not like their mothers and aunts, but like the slender stars of  ‘Melrose Place’ and ‘Beverly Hills 90210’” (Goode).\r\n        No longer do the young women of the island aspire to grow into full bodied women like the generations before them. Now they worry about being too large and different ways they could reduce their weight. A “Fijian girl said her friends tell her that she is too fat, [and she says] ‘sometimes I’m depressed because I always want to lose weight’” (Goode). Beauty standards drastically changed due to Western influence through the media, and witnessing the speed with which these new ideals gripped the young women of this island is troubling. Unfortunately, this phenomenon is not exclusive to India and Fiji, but has in fact been happening all over the world for centuries. \r\nClearly, not only Indian beauty standards, but all beauty standards, can have harmful effects on women who define their self-worth through these ideals. Especially when the beauty standard is treated as objective fact. Obviously, beauty cannot be objective, right? “In recent years, a number of studies have been done… that try to define an objective standard for beauty, to classify exactly what makes someone beautiful in the eyes of others… Many of these studies provide compelling evidence for their claims. But even if these factors contribute to beauty, it would be difficult to claim that they are the sole determiners of what constitutes an attractive appearance” (Gelles 2-3). Obviously, this is because beauty is subjective. There is no one objective ideal, and every culture values different things to different degrees. But its subjectivity also makes it dangerous, because that means a beauty standard can take any form, no matter how unrealistic it might be.\r\nUnfortunately, “the desire of people to conform to an idealized, even unreachable, standard of beauty can have a negative effect on both their physical and mental health” (Gelles 3 - 4). Reaching after an unreachable goal can lead to disappointment, dissatisfaction, and poor mental health for those unsuccessful. “Women’s self-esteem can be affected by poor body image, and they can even experience health problems caused by eating disorders or the use of dangerous chemicals designed, supposedly, to improve their appearance” (Gelles 23). Not only are modern beauty standards harmful to women, but the lasting psychological effects of colonialism continue to affect the people of any colonized nation. Indian women during British rule is just one example of this. Imagine living in your native country and observing how your Eurocentric looking counterparts are consistently treated better than you. You would also undoubtedly develop an inferiority complex towards white people that you would pass down to your children, and your children’s children. \r\nAs much as I’d like to delve into every other example of this, I have to acknowledge that it is beyond the scope of this paper, and instead would like to end with this sentiment: “Any society with the concept of beauty by necessity possesses beauty standards: if you do not have a way to differentiate what is beautiful from what is not, then beauty is meaningless” (Gelles 23).  Beauty is only meaningful because of how a society decides it to be. It’s easy to forget that most  ideals are based on unrealistic standards and often have no justifiable inherent meaning, despite their perceived importance. The real objective definition of beauty should always be self-love and self-acceptance, because nothing else could possibly matter more.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nWorks Cited\r\nChen, Toby, et al. “Occidentalisation of Beauty Standards: Eurocentrism in Asia.” Zenodo.org, International Socioeconomics Library, 16 Dec. 2020, https://projects.iq.harvard.edu/files/isl/files/occidentalisation_of_beauty_standards_eurocentrism.pdf. \r\nFanon, Frantz. Black Skin, White Masks. Pluto Press, 2008. \r\nFenoglio, Isabel. “Colonialism Influenced Beauty Standards, Activist Says - the Heights.” The Heights - The Independent Student Newspaper of Boston College, The Heights, 3 June 2020, https://www.bcheights.com/2017/11/09/colonialism-influenced-beauty-standards-activist-says/. \r\nGelles, Rebecca. “Fair and Lovely: Standards of Beauty, Globalization, and the Modern Indian Woman.” SIT Digital Collections, Carleton College, 2011, https://digitalcollections.sit.edu/isp_collection/1145/. \r\nGoode, Erica. “Study Finds TV Alters Fiji Girls' View of Body.” The New York Times, The New York Times, 20 May 1999, https://www.nytimes.com/1999/05/20/world/study-finds-tv-alters-fiji-girls-view-of-body.html. \r\n♡, Nature [@naturedreamgirl]. “// colorism the way that Indian beauty standards expect desi girls to undergo physical harm in order to be ‘pretty’ is exhausting. dark skinned women turning to steroids, drugs, and other methods in order to be considered pretty is heartbreaking and unacceptable.” Twitter, 15 November 2021, https://twitter.com/naturedreamgirl/status/1460276260198928384 \r\nMajidi, Khesraw. “About Us.” The Lion And The Hunter, 22 June 2020, http://thelionandthehunter.org/norms-of-beauty-in-india-fair-is-beautiful-a-legacy-of-colonialism-and-globalization/#_Toc43152720. \r\nSuden, Ashwinder [@Ashwinder_Suden]. “Colourism and eurocentric beauty standards have robbed billions of people of their ability to feel confident and has created an industry willing to prey on those manufactured insecurities.” Twitter, 3 Jan. 2021, https://mobile.twitter.com/Ashwinder_Suden/status/1345788975076831232. \r\nSuden, Ashwinder [@Ashwinder_Suden]. “Imagine being a dark skinned girl in India. You’d be bombarded with the media portraying women with lighter skin as the peak of beauty. You’d be shown ads for skin bleaching your whole life. You’d be called ‘ugly’ because of your dark skin at some point.” Twitter, 3 Jan. 2021, https://mobile.twitter.com/Ashwinder_Suden/status/1345788975076831232. \r\nReuters. “India's Multibillion-Dollar Skin Lightening Industry under Fire as Indians Seek Whiter Shade of Pale.” FashionNetwork.com, 27 Apr. 2017, https://ww.fashionnetwork.com/news/India-s-multibillion-dollar-skin-lightening-industry-under-fire-as-indians-seek-whiter-shade-of-pale,821354.html. \r\n“The Effect of the British Raj on Clothing in India.” Strand of Silk, 11 Aug. 2013, https://strandofsilk.com/indian-fashion-blog/driven-curiosity/effect-british-raj-clothing-india. \r\nThomas, Aparna. “Western Michigan University ScholarWorks at WMU.” Scholarworks, Western Michigan University, Dec. 1996, https://scholarworks.wmich.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5120&context=masters_theses.", "html": "<p>Nalini Kantheti</p>\n<p>ENGW 1111</p>\n<p>Jeremy Bushnell</p>\n<p>04/15/2022</p>\n<p>Indian Beauty Standards and British Colonization</p>\n<p>How bizarre is it that a community composed almost entirely of medium\nto dark skinned people is obsessed with having fair skin? By ‘obsessed’,\nI mean having a four billion dollar skin bleaching industry with its\nmost popular product called <em>Fair &amp; Lovely</em>\n(Reuters)<em>.</em> It seems ridiculous, right? In India, this\npreference to have lighter skin has been embedded in its culture for\ncenturies. Now, the modern beauty standard not only demands lighter\nskin, but also a slender figure, slimmer nose, lighter eyes, and\ncommends having Eurocentric features in general. I wonder why. It seems\nwe can still see the lasting legacy of colonization reflected in today’s\nIndia, more than a half-century after achieving independence, in the\nmost obvious form of persisting colorism.</p>\n<p>But that’s just one way in which colonization forever changed India’s\ninternal beauty standards, and it’s more informative to examine the\nbigger picture. How were South Asian beauty standards impacted by\nBritish colonization? There are several complex ways in which British\nand Indian cultures reacted to each other, but in short, British\ncolonization impacted Indian beauty standards by further oppressing\nwomen, promoting Eurocentric ideals, and heavily reinforcing\ncolorism.</p>\n<p>However, analyzing these cultural changes in context with the culture\nas a whole can paint a clearer picture. Before British colonization, the\nIndian beauty standard largely followed the natural appearance of its\nadherents, with a few alterations. The first depictions of women seem to\nappear in the form of Mauryan figures around the first to fourth century\nBCE. They “presented full breasts, wide hips and tapered legs”, which\nfit in with other ancient beauty standards as they were often centered\naround fertility. However, around the first century BCE, Sunga figurines\n“contorted the body into S-shaped curves to emphasize sexuality”, and by\nthe Kushan era, these figurines began to feature “eyes that were placed\ntwo-thirds of the way up the face… breast[s that] were places one heads\nheight below the chin… and the use of a fairly round face.” The\nparticularity of these proportions can indicate an idealized beauty\nstandard, and that these were not the natural proportions of Indian\nwomen (Chen 4).</p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/academic/paper-one/media/image2.png\"\nstyle=\"width:1.69792in;height:2.8125in\" /> <img\nsrc=\"assets/academic/paper-one/media/image1.png\"\nstyle=\"width:1.653in;height:2.1945in\" /> <img\nsrc=\"assets/academic/paper-one/media/image3.png\"\nstyle=\"width:1.64063in;height:2.1875in\" /></p>\n<p><em>Mauryan Figure (Majidi) Sunga Figure (Majidi) Kushan Figure\n(Majidi)</em></p>\n<p>Another representation of the Indian beauty ideal stems from the\nportrayal of Hindu goddesses, especially Parvati, the goddess of love,\ndevotion and fertility, who is characterized as “the grand\npersonification of … the concept of beauty”. She is described to be “a\nslender-bodied maiden of comely hips and [a] moon-like face” that is\noften pale or glowing. Moreover, her facial features are described to\nhave “eyes as lotus petals, .. [a] lower lip like the Bimba, … and her\nnose like the beak of a parrot” (Majidi). From these descriptions, it's\nclear that fertility was an influential and recurring theme in ancient\nIndian beauty ideals, with the most desirable qualities being wide hips\nand a round face.</p>\n<p>This beauty ideal did not change much throughout the later centuries,\nas medium complexioned skin color, thick black hair, and a curvy figure\nremained as preferable qualities to have in India. This is evidenced by\na marriage advertisement for a thirteen year old girl in Calcutta in\n1910, in which her family describes her as having a “medium complexion\nskin color, and good figure.” Another girl’s parents in 1927 wrote that\ntheir daughter has an “Ujjwala Shyama varna,” or a ‘glistening dark\ncomplexion’. Additionally, a Bengali writer, Mohitlal Majumdar (1888 -\n1952), characterized the ideal Bengali woman as “medium complexioned,\nwith wide downcast eyes; [with] tresses [that] are black, wavy, and\nflowing… she shows no dearth of modesty and decency” (Majidi).</p>\n<p>From all of these descriptions, we can draw a general and traditional\nbeauty standard that features large eyes, a narrow waist but wider hips\nand breasts, and long black hair. It’s important to note that though\nhaving medium toned or golden skin was most desirable, having dark skin\nwas not necessarily unattractive at the time. For centuries, the beauty\nstandard in India was mostly unchanging and followed some cyclical\nalterations. It is also more closely aligned with the average Indian\nwoman’s appearrance, making it more realistic and attainable.</p>\n<p>Everything changed when the British colonized India. Not only did the\nbeauty standard drastically shift, but the British also affected the\nfundamental role of women in society. Though the new laws and policies\nhad a significant impact on the status of all women, it varied based on\nclass and caste. Poor women suffered a dual loss, as the destruction of\nthe Indian system of industry and agriculture eroded their economic\nposition. Additionally, all women lost their social freedoms “as they\ncame under the restrictive Hindu norms which were now universally\napplied by the British.” In 1722, the governor-general of Bengal,\nWilliam Hastings, “declared Hindu texts as the sole source of legal\nauthority, thus imposing upper caste Hindu norms on lower caste women”\n(Thomas). This meant that Indian women were now expected to follow the\nsocial and moral constraints of those laws. However, it’s important to\nnote that the British only used Hindu laws in personal areas such as\nmarriage, divorce, and inheritance. British parliamentary law came first\nin all other areas. Evidently, the British wanted to further suppress\nIndian women by ruthlessly removing their freedoms, most likely to\nfurther legitimize their rule.</p>\n<p>Not only did these new reforms affect the lives of women, but they\nrestructured entire families as well. For example, “the Nayars of\nMalabar in Kerala maintained a matrilineal form of family structure\nuntil the British imposed legal restrictions in the 19th century. A\nNayar family consisted of a woman, her sisters and brothers and her\nchildren… Neither partner received any rights or duties towards the\nother, however the woman received the right to sexual activity.”\nUnfortunately, the British introduced a number of legal changes that\nundermined the Nayar’s family structure by completely changing the role\nof the father: “In 1868 the British passed a law that a man had to\nprovide for his wife and children… The Nayar father thus gained the\nright to the property of his wife's family” (Thomas). The British\nposition regarding women’s status is certainly not favorable, but this\nseems to be less intentional and more of a disregard for Indian women’s\nwell being in general. Women of all castes were affected by their\nreforms to different degrees, but many suffered the loss of their\neconomic and sexual rights. This set the stage for a new era of India,\nand with it came a new beauty standard to follow.</p>\n<p>As we’ve already established, the ideal Indian beauty standard was\nmedium complexioned skin, a narrow waist but wider hips, round breasts,\nthick black hair, and a moon-like face. The modern beauty standard looks\na little different. As mentioned before, it heavily draws on European\nfeatures, “such as their light skin and light colored eyes.”\nConsequently, “Fair and beautiful are synonymous” (Chen). The new ideal\ndemands lighter skin from women with a historically darker complexion.\nBut that’s not the only thing that changed. “Historical images of Indian\nwomen revealed the preference of curvy hourglass figures and even some\nextra fat in the stomach area, [but] the modern ideal is dominated by\nslim figures” (Majidi). The standard morphed to better suit lighter\nskinned and slim figured women, neither of which are known to be\ntraditional Indian features. It’s more reminiscent of a European woman\nthan an Indian one, which is highly troubling but fairly obvious as to\nwhy.</p>\n<p>Even the clothing that was worn did not escape the ramifications of\nBritish rule. The saree, traditionally worn as one single piece of\nfabric (except upper class women who wore a breast band called a\n‘Pratidhi’) was permanently modified, so that a petticoat or blouse was\nnow needed, presumably for modesty. Who else could have caused this\nchange? “The culture of wearing blouses came from the British.” (The\nEffect). This alteration is still present in modern day sarees, and it\nis often assumed that blouses and petticoats were always a part of the\noutfit, despite the English name. This is so ingrained in Indian culture\nthat its origin is rarely questioned.</p>\n<p>Most disturbingly however, the existing colorism in India thrived\nunder British rule, and grew into an unquenchable monster of an issue\nthat has plagued Indians for decades. “A legacy said to have been left\nby Western imperialism is having fair skin” (Chen). The magnitude of\nthis preference for fair skin can be demonstrated by examining a popular\nbeauty product known as <em>Fair &amp; Lovely</em>. “The fairness cream\nbrand ‘Fair and Lovely’ ha[s] been notorious for perpetuating archaic\nbeauty standards that fairer women achieve more success than darker\nwomen” (Chen). This is an extremely popular skin bleaching cream that\nhas dominated the Indian beauty market for decades. Many of their\nadvertisements feature a ‘dark’ skinned woman whose problems are fixed\nby a lighter skinned woman graciously gifting her some Fair &amp; Lovely\ncream. I remember watching their ads myself at a young age and believing\nthat my superficial problems were due to the darkness of my skin. As\nNatureDreamGirl on Twitter puts it, “dark skinned women turning to\nsteroids, drugs, and other methods in order to be considered pretty is\nheartbreaking and unacceptable.”</p>\n<p>She and I are not alone in reprimanding this phenomenon. Ashwinder\nSuden on Twitter also acknowledges the harmful effects of colorism on\nIndia’s youngest generation: “Imagine being a dark skinned girl in\nIndia. You’d be bombarded with the media portraying women with lighter\nskin as the peak of beauty. You’d be shown ads for skin bleaching your\nentire life. You’d be called ‘ugly’ because of your dark skin at some\npoint… Colourism and Eurocentric beauty standards have robbed billions\nof people of their ability to feel confident and have created an\nindustry willing to prey on those manufactured insecurities.” This is\nthe reality for virtually all dark skinned Indian women, after colorism\ntook root during British rule and seeped into every aspect of life\nsince: beauty, status, and self worth. If you are dark skinned, you are\nautomatically at a disadvantage through no fault of your own.</p>\n<p>Khesraw Majidi, an accomplished Social Sciences masters student at\nHumboldt University shares an anecdote in his article that further\nillustrates how disturbing the importance of skin tone in Indian beauty\nis. While traveling on a train in India, he saw a magazine with some\narticles written by schoolchildren, in which an eleven-year-old girl\nwrites: “I am in 5th class… I like my history teacher because she is\n‘Gori’, (white or fair) she is gorgeous, and a kind woman… And I do not\nlike my math’s teacher because she is ‘Kali’ (black or darker color) and\nshe is ugly and bad”. Think about how you would react if you happened to\ncome across this article. Most Americans will scoff at such blatant\ncolorism, but for people of color, this comes as no surprise. Colorism\nis so deeply rooted in Indian culture that not only is this kind of\nthinking not abnormal, but commonplace.</p>\n<p>Award winning writer, artist, and activist Zahira Kelley explores the\nlasting consequences of colonialism in her lecture at Boston College.\n“She argued that society’s understanding of beauty was profoundly\naltered by colonization and imperialism, which imposed a standard of\n‘whiteness’ to measure beauty. This, she continued, led to the notion\nthat ‘whiter is better’” (Fenoglio). Though not of Indian origin, she\nalso discusses colorism in colored societies. “The beauty standards\nimposed on minorities are also caused by a ‘hierarchy’ within people of\ncolor… Specifically, colorism, which represents discrimination based on\none’s skin tone, establishes these hierarchies within race. Those who\nmost closely resemble Europeans rise to the top of the caste system”\n(Fenoglio). This notion is very present in Indian societies, as those\nwith European features are considered to be the most beautiful and good,\nevidenced by the little girl’s words from the magazine. This notion that\nblack or white somehow correlates to inherent goodness or badness is not\na mistake and is in fact a very intentional byproduct of\ncolonialism.</p>\n<p>However, to truly understand the idea that ‘white is good’ and ‘fair\nis beautiful’ in India, we must take a step back and observe\npostcolonial societies as a whole. Celebrated French Indian philosopher\nand psychiatrist Frantz Fanon explores this idea in his book, <em>Black\nSkin, White Masks.</em> In his book, Fanon argues that not only does\ncolonization reap territorial and economic benefits, but also leaves a\nprofound psychological influence on the colonized society. Fanon\ndescribes colonized people as those “in whose soul an inferiority\ncomplex has been created by the death and burial of its local cultural\noriginality” (Fanon 18). Majidi agrees with and expands upon this idea,\naffirming that “people of color adopt the same values and ideals as the\ninvaders and create a vision that deviates them from their world because\nthey understand they will never and cannot become a white nation.”\nColonialism breeds a mindset of inferiority in the colonized people,\nwhile those who most closely resemble the colonizers are treated better\nand rise to the top. In the case of India, “British colonialists\npromoted this notion by attributing Indians with fairer skin to the\nhigher class and caste, of course while remaining inferior to the\ncolonizers, and the darker Indians to the lower class and caste, ugly\nand cheap people” (Majidi).</p>\n<p>British colonization changed Indian beauty standards in such a\ndrastic way that it still holds influence over them a half century after\nIndian independence. By further subjugating the status of women,\nimprinting Eurocentric standards onto the colonized population, and\nheavily strengthening the existing colorism in India, the British have\nforever changed what it means to be an Indian woman. Consider your\nculture's ideal beauty standard. Imagine how absurd it would be if you\nfound that most of the things that seemed to be ingrained in your\nculture were actually the product of an outsider's influence. This is\nthe pill the modern Indian woman must swallow. She knows that she must\nbleach her skin, wear blouses with her sarees, and maintain a slender\nfigure to be beautiful, even though none of these things ever came\nnaturally to her.</p>\n<p>The most troubling aspect of this issue is that it’s not limited to\nIndia. Consider the island of Fiji, whose beauty standards remained the\nsame until just recently, when televisions were introduced to the\nisland. In previous years, “[a] robust, nicely rounded body [was] the\nnorm for men and women. ‘Skinny legs’ [was] a major insult. And ‘going\nthin’... [was] considered a worrisome condition”. However, after\ntelevisions became widely available in 1995 and young women were\nsubjected to Western media, the beauty ideal drastically shifted. “Just\na few years after the introduction of television… eating disorders are\non the rise among young girls, according to a study presented at the\nAmerican Psychiatric Association meetings in Washington<mark>. Young\ngirls dream of looking not like their mothers and aunts, but like the\nslender stars of ‘Melrose Place’ and ‘Beverly Hills 90210’”\n(Goode).</mark></p>\n<p><mark>No longer do the young women of the island aspire to grow into\nfull bodied women like the generations before them. Now they worry about\nbeing too large and different ways they could reduce their weight. A\n“Fijian girl said her friends tell her that she is too fat, [and she\nsays] ‘sometimes I’m depressed because I always want to lose weight’”\n(Goode).</mark> Beauty standards drastically changed due to Western\ninfluence through the media, and witnessing the speed with which these\nnew ideals gripped the young women of this island is troubling.\nUnfortunately, this phenomenon is not exclusive to India and Fiji, but\nhas in fact been happening all over the world for centuries.</p>\n<p>Clearly, not only Indian beauty standards, but all beauty standards,\ncan have harmful effects on women who define their self-worth through\nthese ideals. Especially when the beauty standard is treated as\nobjective fact. Obviously, beauty cannot be objective, right? “In recent\nyears, a number of studies have been done… that try to define an\nobjective standard for beauty, to classify exactly what makes someone\nbeautiful in the eyes of others… Many of these studies provide\ncompelling evidence for their claims. But even if these factors\ncontribute to beauty, it would be difficult to claim that they are the\nsole determiners of what constitutes an attractive appearance” (Gelles\n2-3). Obviously, this is because beauty is subjective. There is no one\nobjective ideal, and every culture values different things to different\ndegrees. But its subjectivity also makes it dangerous, because that\nmeans a beauty standard can take any form, no matter how unrealistic it\nmight be.</p>\n<p>Unfortunately, “the desire of people to conform to an idealized, even\nunreachable, standard of beauty can have a negative effect on both their\nphysical and mental health” (Gelles 3 - 4). Reaching after an\nunreachable goal can lead to disappointment, dissatisfaction, and poor\nmental health for those unsuccessful. “Women’s self-esteem can be\naffected by poor body image, and they can even experience health\nproblems caused by eating disorders or the use of dangerous chemicals\ndesigned, supposedly, to improve their appearance” (Gelles 23). Not only\nare modern beauty standards harmful to women, but the lasting\npsychological effects of colonialism continue to affect the people of\nany colonized nation. Indian women during British rule is just one\nexample of this. Imagine living in your native country and observing how\nyour Eurocentric looking counterparts are consistently treated better\nthan you. You would also undoubtedly develop an inferiority complex\ntowards white people that you would pass down to your children, and your\nchildren’s children.</p>\n<p>As much as I’d like to delve into every other example of this, I have\nto acknowledge that it is beyond the scope of this paper, and instead\nwould like to end with this sentiment: “Any society with the concept of\nbeauty by necessity possesses beauty standards: if you do not have a way\nto differentiate what is beautiful from what is not, then beauty is\nmeaningless” (Gelles 23). Beauty is only meaningful because of how a\nsociety decides it to be. It’s easy to forget that most ideals are based\non unrealistic standards and often have no justifiable inherent meaning,\ndespite their perceived importance. The real objective definition of\nbeauty should always be self-love and self-acceptance, because nothing\nelse could possibly matter more.</p>\n<p>Works Cited</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Chen, Toby, et al. “Occidentalisation of Beauty Standards:\nEurocentrism in Asia.” <em>Zenodo.org</em>, International Socioeconomics\nLibrary, 16 Dec. 2020,\nhttps://projects.iq.harvard.edu/files/isl/files/occidentalisation_of_beauty_standards_eurocentrism.pdf.</p>\n<p>Fanon, Frantz. <em>Black Skin, White Masks</em>. Pluto Press,\n2008.</p>\n<p>Fenoglio, Isabel. “Colonialism Influenced Beauty Standards, Activist\nSays - the Heights.” <em>The Heights - The Independent Student Newspaper\nof Boston College</em>, The Heights, 3 June 2020,\nhttps://www.bcheights.com/2017/11/09/colonialism-influenced-beauty-standards-activist-says/.</p>\n<p>Gelles, Rebecca. “Fair and Lovely: Standards of Beauty,\nGlobalization, and the Modern Indian Woman.” <em>SIT Digital\nCollections</em>, Carleton College, 2011,\nhttps://digitalcollections.sit.edu/isp_collection/1145/.</p>\n<p>Goode, Erica. “Study Finds TV Alters Fiji Girls' View of Body.”\n<em>The New York Times</em>, The New York Times, 20 May 1999,\nhttps://www.nytimes.com/1999/05/20/world/study-finds-tv-alters-fiji-girls-view-of-body.html.</p>\n<p><mark>♡, Nature [@naturedreamgirl]. “// colorism the way that Indian\nbeauty standards expect desi girls to undergo physical harm in order to\nbe ‘pretty’ is exhausting.</mark> dark skinned women turning to\nsteroids, drugs, and other methods in order to be considered pretty is\nheartbreaking and unacceptable.<mark>” <em>Twitter</em>, 15 November\n2021,\nhttps://twitter.com/naturedreamgirl/status/1460276260198928384</mark></p>\n<p>Majidi, Khesraw. “About Us.” <em>The Lion And The Hunter</em>, 22\nJune 2020,\nhttp://thelionandthehunter.org/norms-of-beauty-in-india-fair-is-beautiful-a-legacy-of-colonialism-and-globalization/#_Toc43152720.</p>\n<p>Suden, Ashwinder [@Ashwinder_Suden]. “Colourism and eurocentric\nbeauty standards have robbed billions of people of their ability to feel\nconfident and has created an industry willing to prey on those\nmanufactured insecurities.” <em>Twitter</em>, 3 Jan. 2021,\nhttps://mobile.twitter.com/Ashwinder_Suden/status/1345788975076831232.</p>\n<p>Suden, Ashwinder [@Ashwinder_Suden]. “Imagine being a dark skinned\ngirl in India. You’d be bombarded with the media portraying women with\nlighter skin as the peak of beauty. You’d be shown ads for skin\nbleaching your whole life. You’d be called ‘ugly’ because of your dark\nskin at some point.” <em>Twitter</em>, 3 Jan. 2021,\nhttps://mobile.twitter.com/Ashwinder_Suden/status/1345788975076831232.</p>\n<p>Reuters. “India's Multibillion-Dollar Skin Lightening Industry under\nFire as Indians Seek Whiter Shade of Pale.” <em>FashionNetwork.com</em>,\n27 Apr. 2017,\nhttps://ww.fashionnetwork.com/news/India-s-multibillion-dollar-skin-lightening-industry-under-fire-as-indians-seek-whiter-shade-of-pale,821354.html.</p>\n<p>“The Effect of the British Raj on Clothing in India.” <em>Strand of\nSilk</em>, 11 Aug. 2013,\nhttps://strandofsilk.com/indian-fashion-blog/driven-curiosity/effect-british-raj-clothing-india.</p>\n<p>Thomas, Aparna. “Western Michigan University ScholarWorks at WMU.”\n<em>Scholarworks</em>, Western Michigan University, Dec. 1996,\nhttps://scholarworks.wmich.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5120&amp;context=masters_theses.</p>\n</blockquote>\n"}, {"id": "paper-two", "sourceTitle": "Revised Persuasion Piece", "text": "Kantheti \r\nNalini Kantheti\r\nENGW 1111\r\nJeremy Bushnell\r\n03/24/2022\r\nIndian Beauty Standards and the Patriarchy\r\n        When I was a young girl, I remember being very confused. So many things about Indian girlhood just didn’t make sense to me. I couldn’t whistle or sit with my legs open or have darkened skin as a result of playing outside, because these things were not ‘ladylike’. I remember thinking to myself, ‘why does being ladylike even matter? I’m just a kid.’ Of course I never said this out loud, because I quickly learned that questioning your ladylike duties is not very ladylike. Now that I’m older, I can confirm that my childhood intuition was correct, and all these ‘ladylike’ rules were ultimately nonsense spewed by men in order to keep a tight grasp on women under their control. This practice of women submitting to men is as old as the first patriarchal society, and is especially prominent in Indian cultures to this day.\r\n        Not only is the practice of having a patriarchal society unnecessary, it is also extremely harmful to the women involved. Just a few consequences of being a women in such a society include a lack of basic rights, incessant bullying, and rape, many of which still occur in the 21st century. Of course, there are several efforts to end this era of patriarchy, and we are slowly making progress, but there are still several unforeseen indirect consequences of having a patriarchal society that are just as detrimental as the direct ones. For instance, centuries of complying to men’s wants and wishes combined with recent technology like social media have developed an unrealistic beauty standard that is impossible to satisfy. No longer do little girls just worry about closing their legs and curbing their whistling, but now they also worry about buying the most fashionable clothes, the inches of their waistline, and the makeup that will enhance their face best. What we have inflicted on our own children is truly troubling, and only highlights the urgency with which we must undo our collective patriarchal mindset.\r\nI’m not alone in endorsing this endeavor. Mumbai-based makeup artist and Maybelline brand ambassador Elton Fernandez feels the same way. Throughout his career in beauty and cosmetics, he has witnessed firsthand how someone’s appearance controls the way they're perceived before they ever say a word. He too agrees that the grasp that patriarchal gender stereotypes hold on beauty standards is ridiculous. Growing up as an effeminate boy, Fernandez was often told that ‘pink is for girls and blue is for boys’, that he wasn’t allowed to have Barbies, and to play with his male peers instead of female ones (Fernandez). Fernandez claims that this level of control is outdated and unnecessary, and I couldn’t agree with him more. \r\n        However, it’s worth examining how we reached this point of criticizing gender stereotypes in the first place, or how the first Indian patriarchal system and its subsequent consequences were spurred. As with most things, this issue is rooted in religion. When Hinduism was first created around 1500 BC, one of its most significant doctrines was that a person’s karma or actions in this life determines his or her status in the next life. Thus, the inherent inequality and hierarchy of the subsequent caste system and gender roles came to be justified by this concept. However, women were not believed to be less than men at this time. In fact, women were treated as true equals to men, in part due to how femininity in general was considered divine because of how revered Hindu goddesses are (Thomas 19 - 22). \r\n        But as all good things do, this era of equality came to an end around 200 BC to 200 AD due to the lawmaker Manu. This man completely changed the status of Indian women, claiming that because of women' s natural temper and heartlessness, they would become disloyal to their husbands and thus had to be constantly guarded. The Laws of Manu, the most famous of the Sanskrit laws, would come to dictate the rules of conduct, the privileges of each division of society (caste), and penalties for inappropriate behavior. This was the start of a centuries long pattern of suppressing women in favor of men in India as well as reinforcing the more negative aspects of the caste system (Thomas 21 - 22).\r\n        Things got more complicated when the British colonized India in 1858. Essentially, the British began to use gender divisions in India as a vehicle for proving their superiority and legitimizing their rule. This power negotiation between the colonizers and colonized men in particular resulted in an institutional failure to protect the rights of women. Their rights were severely limited and they became largely relegated to the domestic sphere. This was also a result of conservative Hindu culture that dictated that the domestic hearth equates to the moral strength of a culture. Women become nothing more than a bargaining piece in the ongoing struggle for power between British and Indian men over the power hierarchy, while staying deprived of an actual voice to say anything about it. (Datar 54 -57).\r\n        However, after India gained independence from Britain in 1976, this event was accompanied by several societal reforms and other consequences. Right after achieving independence, women were caught between the state’s need to devolve communal authority and its paternalistic nature, leaving them bereft during this time (Datar 52). Indian leaders also discouraged female revolutionaries from mobilizing by continuing to reinforce strict gender roles for women and confining them to the home. But feminists in the 1970s challenged the inequality they faced and protested and campaigned to reverse them nonetheless, coming together in organized political groups to fight against dowry deaths, rape, domestic abuse and sati, (the practice of a widow’s self immolation on their husband’s funeral pyre) just to name a few (Desai). In 1980, an anti-rape campaign was launched that led to reforms in some of India’s rape laws (Butalia). Progress was starting to be made, but there was still much to undo.\r\n        Almost fifty years later, in the 21st century, marital rape still remains legal in India. This is truly devastating, as feminists have been fighting for equality for a half-century and Indian women are still treated as their husbands’ property (Frayer). Not only that, but recent technologies have played a role in resurrecting colorism in the Indian sphere by consistently promoting Eurocentric beauty standards. It seems for every step taken forward, we move two steps back. Just as women began to speak with a newly found voice, they were immediately silenced and met with hostility from men in power. Today, the modern married Indian women suffers rape from her own husband while tasked with maintaining her beauty to please her rapist.\r\nFernandez says the way the patriarchy influences Indian beauty standards is horrendous and must be undone. In his piece that was featured on CNN Style, Fernandez argues that though Indian beauty standards are increasingly becoming more global, the patriarchy still holds a tremendous amount of negative influence over them. He starts by acknowledging how modern makeup brands and technology like beauty filters have made it easier for women of color to feel beautiful in their own skin but also expresses his frustration that this ‘expanding horizon’ is limited by the influence of the patriarchy. After sharing his own experience with how male Bollywood actors unfairly treat their female cohorts, he discusses just how much control over the female body the patriarchy has and how common it is in the Indian subcontinent. He concludes by reprimanding gender stereotypes in general, and then assures his audience that the real definition of beauty is self-love.\r\nHowever, the part of this article I’d like to emphasize the most is Fernandez’s point that men often have no problem commenting on every aspect of a woman’s appearance yet are also deeply uncomfortable when experiencing the same thing. This is further illustrated by his observation of a straight cis man who claimed to be comfortable with wearing makeup, but really was only okay with wearing a little concealer and eyeliner. Fernandez also noted that wearing anything more than eyeshadow seemed to make this man question his masculinity, making his claims and his actions contradictory. Sadly, even under the guise of breaking beauty norms, the patriarchy’s grip on our actions and beliefs still holds tight. \r\nWhile speaking on this subject, Fernandez makes an observation that reveals why this is so problematic. After criticizing the way men expect women to adjust every part of themselves to better fit to their liking, Fernandez laments that there often isn’t an end to this madness: “Female actors are constantly seen going under the knife, and for what? To just look prettier or younger, when the real health that matters – mental and physical – may well be crumbling on the inside.” This helps illustrate just how harmful beauty standards the patriarchy imposes on women truly are, as it’s often the case to prioritize physical aesthetics over health for many women as a result. Honestly, the idea that one group can have so much say in how another even views itself is ridiculous, and we must untangle our beauty standards from the way the patriarchy views and treats women.\r\nI strongly agree with Fernandez’s claim that all the nonsense women go through to make themselves more appealing to men is pointless and harmful, simply because I’ve seen it play out in my own life. When I was in middle school, I had several male classmates who consistently criticized my weight and constantly made comments about how unattractive I was in general. Hearing these things almost every day made my impressionable preteen self genuinely believe that the only way I could gain anyone’s respect was to be more ‘beautiful’. So I did. I spent the entire summer after eighth grade eating as little as possible, exercising every day, and promising myself that it would all be worth it when I was finally ‘beautiful’.\r\nTo my surprise, when I made it to ninth grade, one of the very classmates that had told me to ‘lose a few’ the previous year asked me out on a date. I remember wondering, Was that it? That was all I got for changing myself so drastically? I started to feel devastated that my male peers treated me so differently simply because of how I looked, and it made me realize how arbitrary being ‘beautiful’ actually was. Something that I had believed to be wrong with my fundamental character was actually just my male classmates pestering me that they didn’t have something better to look at. This experience ultimately was pointless, as it only served to help me gain the respect of people that I myself didn’t respect anymore due to their disgusting behavior.\r\nNot only did my efforts seem to be in vain, but I had completely destroyed my relationship with food, my self image, and a good chunk of my mental health in the process, and it would take me years to recover from it. For women, their beauty is often something that says a lot about their worth as a person, at least in the eyes of men. But for men, their worth is often based on how smart or talented or charismatic they are. It is utterly unfair that men are judged on their character, while women are judged on such an arbitrary feature. Someone’s perceived beauty should never determine their self worth.\r\nOf course, there are many who argue that men also face similar struggles, just in different ways. Men are expected to be breadwinners of their family, hide their emotions, and be successful in their career. However, it’s vital to understand that even these problems are rooted in the patriarchy. The strict masculine mold imposed on men by a patriarchal society harms both sexes, forcing men to fill a mold they never asked to fill while telling them they’re worth more than women. The ideas that the patriarchy harms women and that men also suffer are not mutually exclusive, but rather both results of a deeper issue. Additionally, discussing men’s suffering doesn’t excuse the hurt that men have inflicted onto women for centuries, but only highlights the harm they have inflicted onto each other and further proves the point that patriarchal societies should not continue to exist.\r\nHowever, it may be puzzling to hear this coming from a female perspective. After all, what could qualify me to speak on male issues if I’ve never experienced them? This argument doesn’t seem fair if I haven’t experienced both sides. Sadly, what qualifies my words is the same thing that qualifies prisoners to speak about their guards. The same thing that qualifies slaves to speak about their masters. When you are being oppressed, you come to know everything about your oppressor, things they may not even know about themselves. It’s advantageous to know every behavior, trigger, and pattern in a predator, for the simple reason of survival. Women have been observing and obeying men for centuries and only now have a voice to share their thoughts. The modern era of feminism is not radical or novel. It has been inevitable ever since the first patriarchal society was created.\r\nIf we want to progress as a society, the next step is to let go of harmful archaic ideals that we simply do not need anymore. Like Fernandez says, it’s time for us to embrace the real definition of beauty: “self-love, sharing and self-acceptance”. It’s time to stop prioritizing arbitrary physical details that please men and focus on loving ourselves as we are. Perhaps patriarchal societies and castes were innovative and kept people cleanly organized in ancient India, but it’s clear that they are no longer necessary to us. Hopefully, with enough combined efforts and gradual change over time, equality among sexes, castes, and races will be achieved. Until then, Fernandez and I urge everyone to realign their beliefs and values, untangle their beauty standards from patriarchal ones, and use new technologies to promote unity rather than division. \r\n________________\r\nWorks Cited\r\nButalia, Urvashi. “Women's Rights Movements in India in 1970s and 1980s.” Women's Rights Movements in India in 1970s and 1980s - The Hindu BusinessLine, The Hindu BusinessLine, 6 Mar. 2020, https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/blink/cover/womens-rights-movements-in-india-in-1970s-and-1980s/article30999015.ece. \r\nDatar, Neil. “The Legacy of Imperialism on Gender Law in India.” Historical Perspectives - Santa Clara University, 2017, https://scholarcommons.scu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1154&context=historical-perspectives. \r\nDesai, Rajvi. “A Brief History of Indian Women Protesting Gender Inequality.” The Swaddle, 19 Oct. 2020, https://theswaddle.com/a-brief-history-of-indian-women-protesting-gender-inequality/. \r\nFernandez, Elton. “India Has an Increasingly Global Perspective on Beauty -- but the Patriarchy Still Looms Large.” CNN, Cable News Network, 10 Mar. 2020, https://www.cnn.com/style/article/elton-fernandez-makeup-beauty-opinion/index.html. \r\nFrayer, Lauren. “Marital Rape Is Still Legal in India. A Court Decision Could Change That.” Npr, 8 Feb. 2022, https://www.npr.org/sections/goatsandsoda/2022/02/08/1047588035/marital-rape-india. \r\nThomas, Aparna. “Gender Consciousness, P Gender Consciousness, Patriarchy and the Indian W Chy and the Indian Women’s Movement .” Western Michigan University ScholarWorks at WMU , Dec. 1996, https://scholarworks.wmich.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5120&context=masters_theses.", "html": "<p>Nalini Kantheti</p>\n<p>ENGW 1111</p>\n<p>Jeremy Bushnell</p>\n<p>03/24/2022</p>\n<p>Indian Beauty Standards and the Patriarchy</p>\n<p>When I was a young girl, I remember being very confused. So many\nthings about Indian girlhood just didn’t make sense to me. I couldn’t\nwhistle or sit with my legs open or have darkened skin as a result of\nplaying outside, because these things were not ‘ladylike’. I remember\nthinking to myself, ‘why does being ladylike even matter? I’m just a\nkid.’ Of course I never said this out loud, because I quickly learned\nthat questioning your ladylike duties is not very ladylike. Now that I’m\nolder, I can confirm that my childhood intuition was correct, and all\nthese ‘ladylike’ rules were ultimately nonsense spewed by men in order\nto keep a tight grasp on women under their control. This practice of\nwomen submitting to men is as old as the first patriarchal society, and\nis especially prominent in Indian cultures to this day.</p>\n<p>Not only is the practice of having a patriarchal society unnecessary,\nit is also extremely harmful to the women involved. Just a few\nconsequences of being a women in such a society include a lack of basic\nrights, incessant bullying, and rape, many of which still occur in the\n21st century. Of course, there are several efforts to end this era of\npatriarchy, and we are slowly making progress, but there are still\nseveral unforeseen indirect consequences of having a patriarchal society\nthat are just as detrimental as the direct ones. For instance, centuries\nof complying to men’s wants and wishes combined with recent technology\nlike social media have developed an unrealistic beauty standard that is\nimpossible to satisfy. No longer do little girls just worry about\nclosing their legs and curbing their whistling, but now they also worry\nabout buying the most fashionable clothes, the inches of their\nwaistline, and the makeup that will enhance their face best. What we\nhave inflicted on our own children is truly troubling, and only\nhighlights the urgency with which we must undo our collective\npatriarchal mindset.</p>\n<p>I’m not alone in endorsing this endeavor. Mumbai-based makeup artist\nand Maybelline brand ambassador Elton Fernandez feels the same way.\nThroughout his career in beauty and cosmetics, he has witnessed\nfirsthand how someone’s appearance controls the way they're perceived\nbefore they ever say a word. He too agrees that the grasp that\npatriarchal gender stereotypes hold on beauty standards is ridiculous.\nGrowing up as an effeminate boy, Fernandez was often told that ‘pink is\nfor girls and blue is for boys’, that he wasn’t allowed to have Barbies,\nand to play with his male peers instead of female ones (Fernandez).\nFernandez claims that this level of control is outdated and unnecessary,\nand I couldn’t agree with him more.</p>\n<p>However, it’s worth examining how we reached this point of\ncriticizing gender stereotypes in the first place, or how the first\nIndian patriarchal system and its subsequent consequences were spurred.\nAs with most things, this issue is rooted in religion. When Hinduism was\nfirst created around 1500 BC, one of its most significant doctrines was\nthat a person’s karma or actions in this life determines his or her\nstatus in the next life. Thus, the inherent inequality and hierarchy of\nthe subsequent caste system and gender roles came to be justified by\nthis concept. However, women were not believed to be less than men at\nthis time. In fact, women were treated as true equals to men, in part\ndue to how femininity in general was considered divine because of how\nrevered Hindu goddesses are (Thomas 19 - 22).</p>\n<p>But as all good things do, this era of equality came to an end around\n200 BC to 200 AD due to the lawmaker Manu. This man completely changed\nthe status of Indian women, claiming that because of women' s natural\ntemper and heartlessness, they would become disloyal to their husbands\nand thus had to be constantly guarded. The Laws of Manu, the most famous\nof the Sanskrit laws, would come to dictate the rules of conduct, the\nprivileges of each division of society (caste), and penalties for\ninappropriate behavior. This was the start of a centuries long pattern\nof suppressing women in favor of men in India as well as reinforcing the\nmore negative aspects of the caste system (Thomas 21 - 22).</p>\n<p>Things got more complicated when the British colonized India in 1858.\nEssentially, the British began to use gender divisions in India as a\nvehicle for proving their superiority and legitimizing their rule. This\npower negotiation between the colonizers and colonized men in particular\nresulted in an institutional failure to protect the rights of women.\nTheir rights were severely limited and they became largely relegated to\nthe domestic sphere. This was also a result of conservative Hindu\nculture that dictated that the domestic hearth equates to the moral\nstrength of a culture. Women become nothing more than a bargaining piece\nin the ongoing struggle for power between British and Indian men over\nthe power hierarchy, while staying deprived of an actual voice to say\nanything about it. (Datar 54 -57).</p>\n<p>However, after India gained independence from Britain in 1976, this\nevent was accompanied by several societal reforms and other\nconsequences. Right after achieving independence, women were caught\nbetween the state’s need to devolve communal authority and its\npaternalistic nature, leaving them bereft during this time (Datar 52).\nIndian leaders also discouraged female revolutionaries from mobilizing\nby continuing to reinforce strict gender roles for women and confining\nthem to the home. But feminists in the 1970s challenged the inequality\nthey faced and protested and campaigned to reverse them nonetheless,\ncoming together in organized political groups to fight against dowry\ndeaths, rape, domestic abuse and sati, (the practice of a widow’s self\nimmolation on their husband’s funeral pyre) just to name a few (Desai).\nIn 1980, an anti-rape campaign was launched that led to reforms in some\nof India’s rape laws (Butalia). Progress was starting to be made, but\nthere was still much to undo.</p>\n<p>Almost fifty years later, in the 21st century, marital rape still\nremains legal in India. This is truly devastating, as feminists have\nbeen fighting for equality for a half-century and Indian women are still\ntreated as their husbands’ property (Frayer). Not only that, but recent\ntechnologies have played a role in resurrecting colorism in the Indian\nsphere by consistently promoting Eurocentric beauty standards. It seems\nfor every step taken forward, we move two steps back. Just as women\nbegan to speak with a newly found voice, they were immediately silenced\nand met with hostility from men in power. Today, the modern married\nIndian women suffers rape from her own husband while tasked with\nmaintaining her beauty to please her rapist.</p>\n<p>Fernandez says the way the patriarchy influences Indian beauty\nstandards is horrendous and must be undone. In his piece that was\nfeatured on <em>CNN Style</em>, Fernandez argues that though Indian\nbeauty standards are increasingly becoming more global, the patriarchy\nstill holds a tremendous amount of negative influence over them. He\nstarts by acknowledging how modern makeup brands and technology like\nbeauty filters have made it easier for women of color to feel beautiful\nin their own skin but also expresses his frustration that this\n‘expanding horizon’ is limited by the influence of the patriarchy. After\nsharing his own experience with how male Bollywood actors unfairly treat\ntheir female cohorts, he discusses just how much control over the female\nbody the patriarchy has and how common it is in the Indian subcontinent.\nHe concludes by reprimanding gender stereotypes in general, and then\nassures his audience that the real definition of beauty is\nself-love.</p>\n<p>However, the part of this article I’d like to emphasize the most is\nFernandez’s point that men often have no problem commenting on every\naspect of a woman’s appearance yet are also deeply uncomfortable when\nexperiencing the same thing. This is further illustrated by his\nobservation of a straight cis man who claimed to be comfortable with\nwearing makeup, but really was only okay with wearing a little concealer\nand eyeliner. Fernandez also noted that wearing anything more than\neyeshadow seemed to make this man question his masculinity, making his\nclaims and his actions contradictory. Sadly, even under the guise of\nbreaking beauty norms, the patriarchy’s grip on our actions and beliefs\nstill holds tight.</p>\n<p>While speaking on this subject, Fernandez makes an observation that\nreveals why this is so problematic. After criticizing the way men expect\nwomen to adjust every part of themselves to better fit to their liking,\nFernandez laments that there often isn’t an end to this madness:\n“<mark>Female actors are constantly seen going under the knife, and for\nwhat?</mark> To just look prettier or younger, when the real health that\nmatters – mental and physical – may well be crumbling on the inside.”\nThis helps illustrate just how harmful beauty standards the patriarchy\nimposes on women truly are, as it’s often the case to prioritize\nphysical aesthetics over health for many women as a result. Honestly,\nthe idea that one group can have so much say in how another even views\nitself is ridiculous, and we must untangle our beauty standards from the\nway the patriarchy views and treats women.</p>\n<p>I strongly agree with Fernandez’s claim that all the nonsense women\ngo through to make themselves more appealing to men is pointless and\nharmful, simply because I’ve seen it play out in my own life. When I was\nin middle school, I had several male classmates who consistently\ncriticized my weight and constantly made comments about how unattractive\nI was in general. Hearing these things almost every day made my\nimpressionable preteen self genuinely believe that the only way I could\ngain anyone’s respect was to be more ‘beautiful’. So I did. I spent the\nentire summer after eighth grade eating as little as possible,\nexercising every day, and promising myself that it would all be worth it\nwhen I was finally ‘beautiful’.</p>\n<p>To my surprise, when I made it to ninth grade, one of the very\nclassmates that had told me to ‘lose a few’ the previous year asked me\nout on a date. I remember wondering, Was that it? That was all I got for\nchanging myself so drastically? I started to feel devastated that my\nmale peers treated me so differently simply because of how I looked, and\nit made me realize how arbitrary being ‘beautiful’ actually was.\nSomething that I had believed to be wrong with my fundamental character\nwas actually just my male classmates pestering me that they didn’t have\nsomething better to look at. This experience ultimately was pointless,\nas it only served to help me gain the respect of people that I myself\ndidn’t respect anymore due to their disgusting behavior.</p>\n<p>Not only did my efforts seem to be in vain, but I had completely\ndestroyed my relationship with food, my self image, and a good chunk of\nmy mental health in the process, and it would take me years to recover\nfrom it. For women, their beauty is often something that says a lot\nabout their worth as a person, at least in the eyes of men. But for men,\ntheir worth is often based on how smart or talented or charismatic they\nare. It is utterly unfair that men are judged on their character, while\nwomen are judged on such an arbitrary feature. Someone’s perceived\nbeauty should never determine their self worth.</p>\n<p>Of course, there are many who argue that men also face similar\nstruggles, just in different ways. Men are expected to be breadwinners\nof their family, hide their emotions, and be successful in their career.\nHowever, it’s vital to understand that even these problems are rooted in\nthe patriarchy. The strict masculine mold imposed on men by a\npatriarchal society harms both sexes, forcing men to fill a mold they\nnever asked to fill while telling them they’re worth more than women.\nThe ideas that the patriarchy harms women and that men also suffer are\nnot mutually exclusive, but rather both results of a deeper issue.\nAdditionally, discussing men’s suffering doesn’t excuse the hurt that\nmen have inflicted onto women for centuries, but only highlights the\nharm they have inflicted onto each other and further proves the point\nthat patriarchal societies should not continue to exist.</p>\n<p>However, it may be puzzling to hear this coming from a female\nperspective. After all, what could qualify me to speak on male issues if\nI’ve never experienced them? This argument doesn’t seem fair if I\nhaven’t experienced both sides. Sadly, what qualifies my words is the\nsame thing that qualifies prisoners to speak about their guards. The\nsame thing that qualifies slaves to speak about their masters. When you\nare being oppressed, you come to know everything about your oppressor,\nthings they may not even know about themselves. It’s advantageous to\nknow every behavior, trigger, and pattern in a predator, for the simple\nreason of survival. Women have been observing and obeying men for\ncenturies and only now have a voice to share their thoughts. The modern\nera of feminism is not radical or novel. It has been inevitable ever\nsince the first patriarchal society was created.</p>\n<p>If we want to progress as a society, the next step is to let go of\nharmful archaic ideals that we simply do not need anymore. Like\nFernandez says, it’s time for us to embrace the real definition of\nbeauty: “self-love, sharing and self-acceptance”. It’s time to stop\nprioritizing arbitrary physical details that please men and focus on\nloving ourselves as we are. Perhaps patriarchal societies and castes\nwere innovative and kept people cleanly organized in ancient India, but\nit’s clear that they are no longer necessary to us. Hopefully, with\nenough combined efforts and gradual change over time, equality among\nsexes, castes, and races will be achieved. Until then, Fernandez and I\nurge everyone to realign their beliefs and values, untangle their beauty\nstandards from patriarchal ones, and use new technologies to promote\nunity rather than division.</p>\n<p>Works Cited</p>\n<p>Butalia, Urvashi. “Women's Rights Movements in India in 1970s and\n1980s.” <em>Women's Rights Movements in India in 1970s and 1980s - The\nHindu BusinessLine</em>, The Hindu BusinessLine, 6 Mar. 2020,\nhttps://www.thehindubusinessline.com/blink/cover/womens-rights-movements-in-india-in-1970s-and-1980s/article30999015.ece.</p>\n<p>Datar, Neil. “The Legacy of Imperialism on Gender Law in India.”\n<em>Historical Perspectives - Santa Clara University</em>, 2017,\nhttps://scholarcommons.scu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1154&amp;context=historical-perspectives.</p>\n<p>Desai, Rajvi. “A Brief History of Indian Women Protesting Gender\nInequality.” <em>The Swaddle</em>, 19 Oct. 2020,\nhttps://theswaddle.com/a-brief-history-of-indian-women-protesting-gender-inequality/.</p>\n<p>Fernandez, Elton. “India Has an Increasingly Global Perspective on\nBeauty -- but the Patriarchy Still Looms Large.” <em>CNN</em>, Cable\nNews Network, 10 Mar. 2020,\nhttps://www.cnn.com/style/article/elton-fernandez-makeup-beauty-opinion/index.html.</p>\n<p>Frayer, Lauren. “Marital Rape Is Still Legal in India. A Court\nDecision Could Change That.” <em>Npr</em>, 8 Feb. 2022,\nhttps://www.npr.org/sections/goatsandsoda/2022/02/08/1047588035/marital-rape-india.</p>\n<p>Thomas, Aparna. “Gender Consciousness, P Gender Consciousness,\nPatriarchy and the Indian W Chy and the Indian Women’s Movement .”\n<em>Western Michigan University ScholarWorks at WMU</em> , Dec. 1996,\nhttps://scholarworks.wmich.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5120&amp;context=masters_theses.</p>\n"}, {"id": "paper-three", "sourceTitle": "Revised Appreciation: The Iron Giant", "text": "Nalini Kantheti\r\nENGW 1111\r\nJeremy Bushnell\r\n02.24.2022\r\nAppreciation Assignment - The Iron Giant\r\n        The first time I came across the film The Iron Giant, It amazed me. It’s so rare to see a movie that excels in artistry, voice acting, and well written characters. I consider it a great piece of art, a masterpiece even. The background art is stunning, the characters are loveable, and the story sticks with you for a long time. However, my memories of the film center around “The Giant,” the true star of the film. A gigantic but gentle robot who can’t remember where he came from who drives the plot of the film and motivates its narrative. I love this movie because I just can’t forget about it, no matter how many years have passed. Not only was it a big part of my childhood, but it continues to influence who I am today.\r\nWhen I first watched this movie and observed this character, he immediately made me feel interested, excited, and comforted. Moreover, the unlikely friendship between this character and the main character, Hogarth Hughes, made me reflect on what friendship means to me, for I didn't realize that I had defined it differently before. To be fair, I first saw the movie at a young age, but it nevertheless caused me to consider the possibility that friendship could be something greater than the sum of its parts. Previously, I had always thought of friendship as more of a static partnership between two people. However, after observing these characters interact with each other, I realized a friendship can exist beyond the people that ignited it. Any viewer can easily see how deeply the Robot and Hogarth care for each other.\r\nFor example, Hogarth goes to great lengths to ensure a home and plenty of food for the Robot and makes him feel like a good person. The Robot also regularly goes out of his way to prioritize Hogarth’s safety, and he protects him diligently. Throughout this movie, both characters uniquely complement each other while learning harsh truths about the world together. This movie showed me that a great friendship takes on a life of its own and almost becomes its own entity, growing and changing along with its constituents, while remaining unique to those who created it. \r\nHowever, you can only appreciate the beauty of this friendship after experiencing the phenomenon yourself. In the movie, you watch these characters grow together. The Robot teaches Hogarth how to be more selfless and caring while the Robot learns how to be a better person and use his power to keep people safe. In my personal friendships, I have definitely watched myself and my friends grow over the years as we learn new things from each other, and have watched our friendship evolve in the process. I have had many experiences with these friends that define how I react to the good and the bad in the world around me. Like the characters in the movie, we have laughed, loved, cried, and grieved together. These friendships have largely shaped who I am today, and I consider them integral to my character. Watching this movie now makes me appreciate the people in my life that much more.\r\nFurthermore, this character makes me think about what it means to act kindly. Later on in the film, townspeople encounter the Robot and believe that he has evil intentions, because they see a weapon created to destroy anything in its path. However, his actions during the whole movie challenge this notion, and the Robot always chooses to spread kindness and peace rather than violence, even after discovering his true identity. This is one of the reasons why I cannot forget about this movie. A long standing debate exists of whether people are inherently good or evil, and one often sees compelling arguments for both sides. I had always concluded from these arguments that ideally everyone would choose to act kindly, but most people act out of fear or self preservation rather than towards a greater good. But this movie really showed me that anyone can be capable of kindness, even in moments when it feels like the world is against you. It breaks your heart to watch such a kind and gentle creature come to terms that he is a weapon that causes death, especially after watching him encounter a dying deer and grieve as Hogarth explains the concept of death to him. \r\nAt the end of the film, the government discovers the Robot and treats him solely as a weapon. They instruct him that his very existence poses a danger to everyone around him and hastily fire a missile  at him and the town in the process. However, the Robot chooses to sacrifice himself by colliding with the missile before it hits the town to save his loved ones and even the people who wanted to kill him moments ago. After witnessing this beautiful moment, I cannot emphasize enough the rattling effect it had on my perception of kindness. \r\nTo discuss some of the Robot’s more formal elements, I will talk about some of the artistic techniques used to portray this story. The rhythm and proportion alone convey a great amount of information about this character. Just the sheer scale of this character compared to his friends emphasizes the inherent dangerousness of the Robot. For example, in a scene where the characters relax at the lake, his cannonball ends up causing a tidal wave that submerges the other characters. He also walks slowly and makes loud metallic thuds with his footsteps, often as a result of his size and clumsiness, but also as an extra effort to move more carefully to not hurt anyone. Despite being so big, he moves with caution and grace, which also creates a sense of safety for the viewer. The form also complements this character’s function spectacularly. The Robot is gentle, compassionate and endearing, so it naturally follows that he has a simple face, big round eyes that glow, and a permanent smile due to the design of his jaw. The character also has such an innocent curiosity about the world around him, and trusts the main character to guide him to the truth, which in turn earns the trust of the watching audience.\r\nIn a broader context, I would compare this robot to a similar one from the television show Lost In Space, which features another robot character that, like the giant, starts out as a weapon and soon becomes a friend to the main character. Since The Iron Giant came out long before Lost In Space, I would not be surprised if he somewhat inspired the other robot character. Both robots serve as destructive weapons until they forget their past and end up befriending a young human child who changes their worldview and their subsequent behavior drastically, even after regaining the memory they had initially lost. Both of these characters kind of fall into the category of a humanized weapon, and both remind us of the existence of humanity in technology and weapons, which admittedly seems pretty contradictory. \r\nIt’s easy to forget that humans are behind nuclear bombs and U-boats, especially when they target you or your loved ones. However, if we remind ourselves that these dangerous weapons were created out of fear and distrust for another, we can start to see them as an extension of our own humanity and not a standalone piece of technology that happens to cause mass destruction. Humans have inflicted tragedy after tragedy on eachother, and still choose to blame it on the weapons of the other side rather than face their own lack of morality. These characters show us that something we don’t understand isn’t always inherently dangerous, and that we must trust others if we want to preserve our humanity.\r\nThis movie appeals to so many viewers because it has a simple yet profound message. There’s a scene in the movie where Hogarth and the Robot are playing together and Hogarth wants him to pretend to be a supervillain, Atomo. However, the Robot refuses and instead insists on being Superman. As we approach newer and newer technology as well as advanced weaponry, movies like The Iron Giant make us step back and consider, do we use our power for good or evil? We have an increasing and alarming amount of weaponry at our fingertips, and though they were created out of fear, they will likely end up causing more fear and chaos with every use. Who will we choose to be, Atomo or Superman?", "html": "<p>Nalini Kantheti</p>\n<p>ENGW 1111</p>\n<p>Jeremy Bushnell</p>\n<p>02.24.2022</p>\n<p>Appreciation Assignment - The Iron Giant</p>\n<p>The first time I came across the film <em>The Iron Giant,</em> It\namazed me. It’s so rare to see a movie that excels in artistry, voice\nacting, and well written characters. I consider it a great piece of art,\na masterpiece even. The background art is stunning, the characters are\nloveable, and the story sticks with you for a long time. However, my\nmemories of the film center around “The Giant,” the true star of the\nfilm. A gigantic but gentle robot who can’t remember where he came from\nwho drives the plot of the film and motivates its narrative. I love this\nmovie because I just can’t forget about it, no matter how many years\nhave passed. Not only was it a big part of my childhood, but it\ncontinues to influence who I am today.</p>\n<p>When I first watched this movie and observed this character, he\nimmediately made me feel interested, excited, and comforted. Moreover,\nthe unlikely friendship between this character and the main character,\nHogarth Hughes, made me reflect on what friendship means to me, for I\ndidn't realize that I had defined it differently before. To be fair, I\nfirst saw the movie at a young age, but it nevertheless caused me to\nconsider the possibility that friendship could be something greater than\nthe sum of its parts. Previously, I had always thought of friendship as\nmore of a static partnership between two people. However, after\nobserving these characters interact with each other, I realized a\nfriendship can exist beyond the people that ignited it. Any viewer can\neasily see how deeply the Robot and Hogarth care for each other.</p>\n<p>For example, Hogarth goes to great lengths to ensure a home and\nplenty of food for the Robot and makes him feel like a good person. The\nRobot also regularly goes out of his way to prioritize Hogarth’s safety,\nand he protects him diligently. Throughout this movie, both characters\nuniquely complement each other while learning harsh truths about the\nworld together. This movie showed me that a great friendship takes on a\nlife of its own and almost becomes its own entity, growing and changing\nalong with its constituents, while remaining unique to those who created\nit.</p>\n<p>However, you can only appreciate the beauty of this friendship after\nexperiencing the phenomenon yourself. In the movie, you watch these\ncharacters grow together. The Robot teaches Hogarth how to be more\nselfless and caring while the Robot learns how to be a better person and\nuse his power to keep people safe. In my personal friendships, I have\ndefinitely watched myself and my friends grow over the years as we learn\nnew things from each other, and have watched our friendship evolve in\nthe process. I have had many experiences with these friends that define\nhow I react to the good and the bad in the world around me. Like the\ncharacters in the movie, we have laughed, loved, cried, and grieved\ntogether. These friendships have largely shaped who I am today, and I\nconsider them integral to my character. Watching this movie now makes me\nappreciate the people in my life that much more.</p>\n<p>Furthermore, this character makes me think about what it means to act\nkindly. Later on in the film, townspeople encounter the Robot and\nbelieve that he has evil intentions, because they see a weapon created\nto destroy anything in its path. However, his actions during the whole\nmovie challenge this notion, and the Robot always chooses to spread\nkindness and peace rather than violence, even after discovering his true\nidentity. This is one of the reasons why I cannot forget about this\nmovie. A long standing debate exists of whether people are inherently\ngood or evil, and one often sees compelling arguments for both sides. I\nhad always concluded from these arguments that ideally everyone would\nchoose to act kindly, but most people act out of fear or self\npreservation rather than towards a greater good. But this movie really\nshowed me that anyone can be capable of kindness, even in moments when\nit feels like the world is against you. It breaks your heart to watch\nsuch a kind and gentle creature come to terms that he is a weapon that\ncauses death, especially after watching him encounter a dying deer and\ngrieve as Hogarth explains the concept of death to him.</p>\n<p>At the end of the film, the government discovers the Robot and treats\nhim solely as a weapon. They instruct him that his very existence poses\na danger to everyone around him and hastily fire a missile at him and\nthe town in the process. However, the Robot chooses to sacrifice himself\nby colliding with the missile before it hits the town to save his loved\nones and even the people who wanted to kill him moments ago. After\nwitnessing this beautiful moment, I cannot emphasize enough the rattling\neffect it had on my perception of kindness.</p>\n<p>To discuss some of the Robot’s more formal elements, I will talk\nabout some of the artistic techniques used to portray this story. The\nrhythm and proportion alone convey a great amount of information about\nthis character. Just the sheer scale of this character compared to his\nfriends emphasizes the inherent dangerousness of the Robot. For example,\nin a scene where the characters relax at the lake, his cannonball ends\nup causing a tidal wave that submerges the other characters. He also\nwalks slowly and makes loud metallic thuds with his footsteps, often as\na result of his size and clumsiness, but also as an extra effort to move\nmore carefully to not hurt anyone. Despite being so big, he moves with\ncaution and grace, which also creates a sense of safety for the viewer.\nThe form also complements this character’s function spectacularly. The\nRobot is gentle, compassionate and endearing, so it naturally follows\nthat he has a simple face, big round eyes that glow, and a permanent\nsmile due to the design of his jaw. The character also has such an\ninnocent curiosity about the world around him, and trusts the main\ncharacter to guide him to the truth, which in turn earns the trust of\nthe watching audience.</p>\n<p>In a broader context, I would compare this robot to a similar one\nfrom the television show <em>Lost In Space</em>, which features another\nrobot character that, like the giant, starts out as a weapon and soon\nbecomes a friend to the main character. Since <em>The Iron Giant</em>\ncame out long before <em>Lost In Space,</em> I would not be surprised if\nhe somewhat inspired the other robot character. Both robots serve as\ndestructive weapons until they forget their past and end up befriending\na young human child who changes their worldview and their subsequent\nbehavior drastically, even after regaining the memory they had initially\nlost. Both of these characters kind of fall into the category of a\nhumanized weapon, and both remind us of the existence of humanity in\ntechnology and weapons, which admittedly seems pretty contradictory.</p>\n<p>It’s easy to forget that humans are behind nuclear bombs and U-boats,\nespecially when they target you or your loved ones. However, if we\nremind ourselves that these dangerous weapons were created out of fear\nand distrust for another, we can start to see them as an extension of\nour own humanity and not a standalone piece of technology that happens\nto cause mass destruction. Humans have inflicted tragedy after tragedy\non eachother, and still choose to blame it on the weapons of the other\nside rather than face their own lack of morality. These characters show\nus that something we don’t understand isn’t always inherently dangerous,\nand that we must trust others if we want to preserve our humanity.</p>\n<p>This movie appeals to so many viewers because it has a simple yet\nprofound message. There’s a scene in the movie where Hogarth and the\nRobot are playing together and Hogarth wants him to pretend to be a\nsupervillain, Atomo. However, the Robot refuses and instead insists on\nbeing Superman. As we approach newer and newer technology as well as\nadvanced weaponry, movies like <em>The Iron Giant</em> make us step back\nand consider, do we use our power for good or evil? We have an\nincreasing and alarming amount of weaponry at our fingertips, and though\nthey were created out of fear, they will likely end up causing more fear\nand chaos with every use. Who will we choose to be, Atomo or\nSuperman?</p>\n"}, {"id": "paper-four", "sourceTitle": "Laughter and Human Nature: Literature Review", "text": "Laughter and Human Nature\r\nNalini Kantheti\r\n\r\n\r\nAbstract: Laughter has always been a core part of human nature. In this paper, we explore the relationship between laughter and the expression of the true self. To begin, we define two types of laughter (Duchenne and non-Duchenne), delve into the history behind why we laugh, dissect the connection between laughter and comedy, and discuss the role of laughter in the face of death and grief. Overall this paper aims to synthesize existing literature in the field to emphasize the importance of embracing our true selves and laughing freely, for laughter connects us to our vitality and authenticity.\r\n\r\n\r\nKeywords: laughter, primates, grief, true self,  comedy, evolution,  human nature\r\n\r\n\r\n1. Introduction \r\n“Become who you are”\r\n-Friedrich Nietzsche\r\n\r\n\r\nMany of us are already aware of a dichotomy within ourselves: our true self and the fake self. If you’re guilty of fibbing about how fashionable a friend’s new boots are or pretending to like the band of your crush, do not fret. Our fake selves form as early as infancy, when we learn what behaviors please our mothers and award us the most affection from them [1]. This is the self we curate for others. Underneath this outward persona lays who we are deep inside, the unconscious, unsocialized version of us. \r\nFascinatingly, how we laugh says much about these two selves. There are two types of laughter: Duchenne Laughter and non-Duchenne Laughter. Duchenne Laughter is “the full bodied outburst that overtakes you… it’s when you don’t know why you’re laughing, but you can’t stop.” [2] The kind of laughter when you don’t care about what facial expression you’re making and when you can barely control the sounds coming out of your mouth [3,4]. Non-Duchenne Laughter is actually much more common, and is characterized as social laughter [2]. This laughter occurs when you laugh out of politeness, awkwardness, or at jokes you don’t get but pretend you do. The purpose of this paper is to dissect and synthesize the existing literature around how laughter (or more specifically, Duchenne laughter) relates to the expression of the true self.  This paper aims to explain why we should always strive to become the true, realized versions of ourselves and laugh a little harder the next time something tickles our funny bone. To do this, I’ll shed some light on how laughter came to be, discuss the role laughter plays in comedy and vice versa, and the surprising relationship between laughter and grief. \r\n2. Origins of Laughter: We’re all Monkeys\r\nLaughter is far from exclusively human. In fact, one doesn’t have to look far to find our laughing animal neighbors. Laughing behavior is observed in a large variety of primates: apes, chimpanzees, gorillas, barbary macaques, orangutans and so on [3-6]. Even more interestingly, the phylogenetically closer the species is to human, the closer to human their laughs appear [5]. Humans laugh because monkeys laugh, and we still have some monkey left in us. But why do monkeys laugh?\r\n2.1 Why Primates Laugh\r\nIt’s thought that laughter in primates started from social grooming. Scientists Barrett, Dunbar and Lycett noticed that both laughter and social grooming release endogenous opiates, and speculated that the enjoyment associated with humor eventually replaced the pleasure associated with grooming [5]. Additionally, after observing tickling during play evoking a laugh-like vocalization in young chimpanzees, Charles Darwin speculated that the areas most vulnerable to tickling (e.g. the neck, abdomen and soles of the feet) were also the most susceptible to predator attack. Tickling may have served as a ‘mock attack’ and therefore could have been evolutionarily adaptive [5]. Ultimately, theorists have reasoned that the ultimate basis for laughter is that it reinforces social activities and facilitates skill development which increases reproductive fitness [4]. \r\n2.2 Laughter in Early Humans\r\nAfter moving on from the jungle, laughter continued to follow humans throughout history. Humor and laughter began to evolve alongside us as we developed more advanced cognitive mechanisms [5]. There are documented humorous conversions by Australian aboriginals and descriptions of professional jesters in Ancient Greek Texts [5]. Laughter has always been deeply intertwined with human nature. To our knowledge, no culture exists that is unfamiliar with laughter [5]. \r\n2.3 Laughter is Animalistic\r\nIn a way, laughter is like any other bodily function, like vomiting or crying or yawning. It’s a forceful expulsion of sound instead of a nastier physical alternative, a “seizure-like activity” that makes your stomach hurt the same way vomiting does [5]. Additionally, like yawning, crying and  vomiting (in the worse cases), laughter is utterly contagious. Obviously, this is far from novel ; most people have experienced such an episode of laughter, where it seems impossible to stop because of how funny the other person’s laughter is. In fact, with enough people, this behavior can incite a laughing epidemic, where whole communities find it extremely difficult to stop laughing. A prime example of this is the Tanzanian laughing epidemic of 1962, where over two hundred adults were overcome with recurrent bouts of hysterical laughter and crying over a period of two months [1,5]. Clearly, laughter is no joke.\r\n2.4 Laughter in Current Society\r\nIn the modern day, we don’t see much of the animalistic, uninhibited Duchenne laughter. Most of the laughter that makes up social interactions is non-Duchenne laughter. 90% of the laughter in our day to day conversations is neat, polite packaged chuckles that punctuate our speech [1]. This isn’t necessarily a bad thing ; non-Duchenne laughter increases the cohesiveness of groups and facilitates social functions. However, because of this, there’s been a waning tolerance of Duchenne laughter in social settings.  Even just in America, it’s pretty commonplace to cover your mouth while laughing. Several Eastern countries like Poland, India, and China perceive laughing and smiling as a sign of stupidity [4]. In Norway, if a stranger on the street smiles at you, it’s safe to assume that they are drunk, insane, American, or all of the above [4]. Many modern cultures prefer laughter to be repressed for the sake of politeness, looking sane, or even safety.\r\n3. Laughter and Comedy: Try not to Bomb\r\nWhen you think of laughter, you think of comedy, and vice versa. You can’t have one without the other. Comedy is the literary and dramatic impetus for laughter [8]. From Shakespeare’s plays to Friends to  John Mulaney’s standup shows, we all have a guilty pleasure that makes us giggle. However, given how easy it is to laugh at something funny, it’s extremely difficult to make someone else laugh. It’s a struggle standup comics, showrunners and playwrights face on the daily: Being funny. \r\n3.1 What makes us Laugh\r\nMost of us strive to be funny. There’s always a grudging respect every teacher has for their class clown, and being funny in the workplace is usually more likely to get you promoted. But what actually makes us laugh? \r\nIt’s an age old question that Nuar Alsadir, author of ‘Animal Joy: A Book of Laughter and Rescuscitation’ and alumni of Clown school, attempts to answer. From her own experience trying to make people laugh and observing others laugh, she says that honesty is what reliably provokes laughter in an audience [2]. Things that show a comic’s underlying humanity, without necessarily being humorous in nature [2]. This is backed up by the findings of Robert Provine,  the man who literally wrote the book on laughter, which stated that less than 20% of the laughter in his observational study was in response to anything resembling a form of humor [1]. Most of the laughter he observed followed banal remarks such as “Look, it’s Andre”, or jokes that definitely wouldn’t kill in a club, like “You don’t have to drink, just buy us drinks” or “Do you date within your species?” [1]. This theory is even corroborated by my own experiences, like one time in my Advanced Writing class when my professor asked a student how he was feeling about his literature review and he answered, “I’m feeling… bad” and the rest of the class chuckled. None of these pre-laugh comments were trying to be funny, but their blunt honesty or expression of humanity evoked laughter nonetheless. Furthermore, there is some merit to the idea that humor and laughter originate in repressed expression of aggressive feelings ; the idea that the deepest laughs come from the release of psychic tension related to inhibited unconscious aggressive impulses  [5]. So it makes sense that what makes us laugh the most is when the deepest parts of our humanity are laid bare, when we can finally indulge in what we spend most of our time hiding away.\r\nThere is another popular theory of what makes something funny, called Incongruity Theory. This theory posits that humor is humorous because it contains two incrongruous elements, where one element is socially normal and the other constitutes a violation of the ‘subjective moral order’ [5, 6, 8]. This can be exemplified by the following joke: a child says to her mother, “Mommy, Mommy! What is a delinquent child?” and the mother replies, “Shut up and pass me the crowbar” [5]. There are many shared gripes regarding the theory, mainly that there is no agreed definition for the term incongruity and that the theory isn’t comprehensive enough to explain all cases of laughter [6,8].\r\n3.2 Audience Laughter\r\nThere are two types of audience laughter in response to comedic media: controlled and uncontrolled laughter. You will not find these definitions anywhere else, because I made them up. Controlled audience laughter occurs when the audience is cued to laugh, while uncontrolled audience laughter occurs when the audience is free to laugh as much or as little (much to the dismay of standup comics) as they want. A classic example of controlled laughter is laugh tracks in older sitcoms: attempts to stimulate contagious laughter in viewers [1, 8]. The use of this device in television can be seen as an attempt to close down alternative readings of the content, suggesting that if you’re not laughing with the track, you’re the only one [8]. In contrast, the audiences in standup comedy shows control how funny the act is, and laugh out of genuine amusement rather than social obligation. In both cases, however, the audience takes on the role of a single entity, laughing together or not at all. Funnily enough, the contrasting types of audience laughter reflect the contrast between our fake and real selves. In canned laugh tracks, the jokes feels disingenuous and forced while the true funniest moments are the naturally occuring unexpected ones. Adam Conover, a standup comedian on CollegeHumor, remarked on his podcast that the biggest laugh he received on stage was in response to a comment he made on an audience member’s eccentric behavior, in the moment [9].\r\n4. Laughter and Death: It’s Kinda Funny\r\nLaughter and Death seem like they couldn’t have less in common. Like light and dark, white and black, they appear as polar opposites. However, like yin and yang, opposites attract. Laughing while processing death is actually quite common among mourners, which actually makes sense considering how much more tempting it is to laugh when the setting is inappropriate. \r\n4.1 Laughter during bereavement\r\nThe surprising abundance of laughter during grief implies that laughter may potentially serve a salutary function in the early months after a loss [10]. Though at odds with traditional bereavement theories that emphasize the importance of working through the emotional pain of a loss, Duchenne laughter predicts improved long-term functioning during bereavement [8, 10]. As mentioned before, laughter can facilitate a release of pent up repressed emotions that may very well relate to complex emotions around the loss of a loved one [5]. \r\n4.2 Inappropriate Laughter\r\nLaughter at a funeral isn’t the only time laughter can feel out of place. Laughter can be used in a variety of contexts, and not always for beneficial reasons. Consider laughter that ostracizes others or laughter associated with mental illness [5].  Bipolar disorder patients have a greater propensity to initiate and enjoy humor during manic episodes [5]. Laughter can cut deep like a knife when it's directed against you, or make you look insane to others when displayed in a strange way. Both of these serve to illustrate just how powerful this tool is in our society, and how vulnerable it makes us. In one way or another, we live at the mercy of laughter, whether it’s directed at us or whether we want to hide it. \r\n5. Discussion\r\nIn this paper, we discussed the history of laughter, laughter and comedy, and laughter and death, as well as how different areas of laughter relate to our true and fake selves. The purpose of this piece was to synthesize the existing research and literature on laughter and its complicated relationship with humans and human nature itself. Ultimately, I hope this paper serves as a reminder to laugh more freely. The next time you enter a laughing fit, let the tears flow, contort your face, double over, and pay no attention to the bizarre sounds leaving your mouth. Laughter shakes us out of our deadness ; When you are in touch with your true self, you feel more alive, present, and embodied [2]. We should all embrace the animalistic, spontaneous, less refined side of ourselves, our true selves. Only then can we become who we are. \r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nReferences\r\n[1] R. Provine, \"Laughter: A Scientific Investigation,\" 1st ed. New York, NY, USA: Penguin Books, 2000.\r\n[2] N. Alsadir, \"Animal Joy,\" 1st ed. Minneapolis, MN, USA: Coffee House Press, 2019.\r\n[3] G. Greengross and R. Mankoff, \"Inside 'Inside Jokes': The hidden side of humor. A review of Matthew M. Hurley, Daniel C. Dennett, and Reginald B. Adams Jr., Inside jokes: Using humor to reverse-engineer the mind,\" Evolutionary Psychology: An International Journal of Evolutionary Approaches to Psychology and Behavior, vol. 10, pp. 443-456, Jan. 2012.\r\n[4] P. K. Whalen, \"The emotional and physiological structure of laughter: A comparison of three kinds of laughs (Antiphonal, Duchenne, and Voiced) and individual differences in the use of laughter in middle-aged and older marriages,\"  UC Berkeley, ProQuest ID: Whalen_berkeley_0028E_10301. Merritt ID: ark:/13030/m5kh0s7g. Retrieved from: https://escholarship.org/uc/item/4dv197h9.\r\n[5] J. Polimeni and J. Reiss, \"The First Joke: Exploring the Evolutionary Origins of Humor,\" Evolutionary Psychology, vol. 4, pp. 1-29, Jan. 2006. doi: 10.1177/147470490600400129.\r\n[6] D. Keltner and G. Bonanno, \"A study of laughter and dissociation: Distinct correlates of laughter and smiling during bereavement,\" Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, vol. 73, no. 4, pp. 687-702, Nov. 1997. doi: 10.1037/0022-3514.73.4.687.\r\n[7] K. Krys, K. Hansen, C. Xing, P. Szarota, and M. Yang, \"Do Only Fools Smile at Strangers? Cultural Differences in Social Perception of Intelligence of Smiling Individuals,\" Journal of Cross-Cultural Psychology, vol. 45, no. 2, pp. 314-321, Feb. 2014. doi: 10.1177/0022022113513922.\r\n[8] H. Baber, J. Donnelly, and J. Morreall, \"Taking Laughter Seriously,\" Philosophy and Phenomenological Research, vol. 45, no. 2, pp. 290-303, Dec. 1984. doi: 10.2307/2107432.\r\n[9] A. Conover, \"Why Do We Laugh? with Nuar Alsadir,\" Factually, Podcast, March 15, 2023. [Online]. Available: https://www.podchaser.com/podcasts/factually-with-adam-conover-853712/episodes/why-do-we-laugh-with-nuar-alsa-166704135. [Accessed: June 6, 2023].\r\n[10] G. Bonanno, \"Laughter during bereavement,\" Bereavement Care, vol. 18, no. 2, pp. 19-22, 1999. doi: 10.1080/02682629908657458.", "html": "<p><strong>Laughter and Human Nature</strong></p>\n<p>Nalini Kantheti</p>\n<p><em><strong>Abstract:</strong> Laughter has always been a core part\nof human nature. In this paper, we explore the relationship between\nlaughter and the expression of the true self. To begin, we define two\ntypes of laughter (Duchenne and non-Duchenne), delve into the history\nbehind why we laugh, dissect the connection between laughter and comedy,\nand discuss the role of laughter in the face of death and grief. Overall\nthis paper aims to synthesize existing literature in the field to\nemphasize the importance of embracing our true selves and laughing\nfreely, for laughter connects us to our vitality and\nauthenticity.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Keywords:</strong> laughter, primates, grief, true self,\ncomedy, evolution, human nature</em></p>\n<p><strong>1. Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>“Become who you are”</p>\n<p>-<em>Friedrich Nietzsche</em></p>\n<p>Many of us are already aware of a dichotomy within ourselves: our\n<em>true self</em> and the <em>fake self.</em> If you’re guilty of\nfibbing about how fashionable a friend’s new boots are or pretending to\nlike the band of your crush, do not fret. Our fake selves form as early\nas infancy, when we learn what behaviors please our mothers and award us\nthe most affection from them [1]. This is the self we curate for others.\nUnderneath this outward persona lays who we are deep inside, the\nunconscious, unsocialized version of us.</p>\n<p>Fascinatingly, how we laugh says much about these two selves. There\nare two types of laughter: Duchenne Laughter and non-Duchenne Laughter.\n<em><strong>Duchenne Laughter</strong></em> is “the full bodied outburst\nthat overtakes you… it’s when you don’t know why you’re laughing, but\nyou can’t stop.” [2] The kind of laughter when you don’t care about what\nfacial expression you’re making and when you can barely control the\nsounds coming out of your mouth [3,4]. <em><strong>Non-Duchenne\nLaughter</strong></em> is actually much more common, and is\ncharacterized as <em>social</em> laughter [2]. This laughter occurs when\nyou laugh out of politeness, awkwardness, or at jokes you don’t get but\npretend you do. The purpose of this paper is to dissect and synthesize\nthe existing literature around how laughter (or more specifically,\n<em>Duchenne laughter</em>) relates to the expression of the true self.\nThis paper aims to explain why we should always strive to become the\ntrue, realized versions of ourselves and laugh a little harder the next\ntime something tickles our funny bone. To do this, I’ll shed some light\non how laughter came to be, discuss the role laughter plays in comedy\nand vice versa, and the surprising relationship between laughter and\ngrief.</p>\n<p><strong>2.</strong> <strong>Origins of Laughter: We’re all\nMonkeys</strong></p>\n<p>Laughter is far from exclusively human. In fact, one doesn’t have to\nlook far to find our laughing animal neighbors. Laughing behavior is\nobserved in a large variety of primates: apes, chimpanzees, gorillas,\nbarbary macaques, orangutans and so on [3-6]. Even more interestingly,\nthe phylogenetically closer the species is to human, the closer to human\ntheir laughs appear [5]. Humans laugh because monkeys laugh, and we\nstill have some monkey left in us. But why do monkeys laugh?</p>\n<p><strong>2.1 Why Primates Laugh</strong></p>\n<p>It’s thought that laughter in primates started from <em>social\ngrooming</em>. Scientists Barrett, Dunbar and Lycett noticed that both\nlaughter and social grooming release endogenous opiates, and speculated\nthat the enjoyment associated with humor eventually replaced the\npleasure associated with grooming [5]. Additionally, after observing\n<strong>tickling</strong> during play evoking a laugh-like vocalization\nin young chimpanzees, Charles Darwin speculated that the areas most\nvulnerable to tickling (e.g. the neck, abdomen and soles of the feet)\nwere also the most susceptible to predator attack. Tickling may have\nserved as a ‘mock attack’ and therefore could have been evolutionarily\nadaptive [5]. Ultimately, theorists have reasoned that the ultimate\nbasis for laughter is that it reinforces social activities and\nfacilitates skill development which increases reproductive fitness\n[4].</p>\n<p><strong>2.2 Laughter in Early Humans</strong></p>\n<p>After moving on from the jungle, laughter continued to follow humans\nthroughout history. Humor and laughter began to evolve alongside us as\nwe developed more advanced cognitive mechanisms [5]. There are\ndocumented humorous conversions by Australian aboriginals and\ndescriptions of professional jesters in Ancient Greek Texts [5].\nLaughter has always been deeply intertwined with human nature. To our\nknowledge, no culture exists that is unfamiliar with laughter [5].</p>\n<p><strong>2.3 <em>Laughter is Animalistic</em></strong></p>\n<p>In a way, laughter is like any other bodily function, like vomiting\nor crying or yawning. It’s a forceful expulsion of <em>sound</em>\ninstead of a nastier physical alternative, a “seizure-like activity”\nthat makes your stomach hurt the same way vomiting does [5].\nAdditionally, like yawning, crying and vomiting (in the worse cases),\nlaughter is utterly contagious. Obviously, this is far from novel ; most\npeople have experienced such an episode of laughter, where it seems\nimpossible to stop because of how funny the other person’s laughter is.\nIn fact, with enough people, this behavior can incite a\n<em><strong>laughing epidemic</strong>,</em> where whole communities\nfind it extremely difficult to stop laughing. A prime example of this is\nthe <em>Tanzanian laughing epidemic of 1962</em>, where over two hundred\nadults were overcome with recurrent bouts of hysterical laughter and\ncrying over a period of two months [1,5]. Clearly, laughter is no\njoke.</p>\n<p><strong>2.4 Laughter in Current Society</strong></p>\n<p>In the modern day, we don’t see much of the animalistic, uninhibited\n<em>Duchenne laughter.</em> Most of the laughter that makes up social\ninteractions is <em>non-Duchenne</em> laughter. 90% of the laughter in\nour day to day conversations is neat, polite packaged chuckles that\npunctuate our speech [1]. This isn’t necessarily a bad thing ;\n<em>non-Duchenne laughte</em>r increases the cohesiveness of groups and\nfacilitates social functions. However, because of this, there’s been a\nwaning tolerance of <em>Duchenne laughter</em> in social settings. Even\njust in America, it’s pretty commonplace to cover your mouth while\nlaughing. Several Eastern countries like Poland, India, and China\nperceive laughing and smiling as a sign of stupidity [4]. In Norway, if\na stranger on the street smiles at you, it’s safe to assume that they\nare drunk, insane, American, or all of the above [4]. Many modern\ncultures prefer laughter to be repressed for the sake of politeness,\nlooking sane, or even safety.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Laughter and Comedy: Try not to Bomb</strong></p>\n<p>When you think of laughter, you think of comedy, and vice versa. You\ncan’t have one without the other. Comedy is the literary and dramatic\nimpetus for laughter [8]. From Shakespeare’s plays to <em>Friends</em>\nto John Mulaney’s standup shows, we all have a guilty pleasure that\nmakes us giggle. However, given how easy it is to laugh at something\nfunny, it’s extremely difficult to make someone else laugh. It’s a\nstruggle standup comics, showrunners and playwrights face on the daily:\n<em>Being</em> funny.</p>\n<p><strong>3.1 What makes us Laugh</strong></p>\n<p>Most of us strive to be funny. There’s always a grudging respect\nevery teacher has for their class clown, and being funny in the\nworkplace is usually more likely to get you promoted. But what actually\nmakes us laugh?</p>\n<p>It’s an age old question that <strong>Nuar Alsadir</strong>, author\nof ‘<em>Animal Joy: A Book of Laughter and Rescuscitation</em>’ and\nalumni of Clown school, attempts to answer. From her own experience\ntrying to make people laugh and observing others laugh, she says that\nhonesty is what reliably provokes laughter in an audience [2]. Things\nthat show a comic’s underlying humanity, without necessarily being\nhumorous in nature [2]. This is backed up by the findings of\n<strong>Robert Provine</strong>, the man who literally wrote the book on\nlaughter, which stated that less than 20% of the laughter in his\nobservational study was in response to anything resembling a form of\nhumor [1]. Most of the laughter he observed followed banal remarks such\nas “Look, it’s Andre”, or jokes that definitely wouldn’t kill in a club,\nlike “You don’t have to drink, just buy us drinks” or “Do you date\nwithin your species?” [1]. This theory is even corroborated by my own\nexperiences, like one time in my Advanced Writing class when my\nprofessor asked a student how he was feeling about his literature review\nand he answered, “I’m feeling… <em>bad”</em> and the rest of the class\nchuckled. None of these pre-laugh comments were <em>trying</em> to be\nfunny, but their blunt honesty or expression of humanity evoked laughter\nnonetheless. Furthermore, there is some merit to the idea that humor and\nlaughter originate in repressed expression of aggressive feelings ; the\nidea that the deepest laughs come from the release of psychic tension\nrelated to inhibited unconscious aggressive impulses [5]. So it makes\nsense that what makes us laugh the most is when the deepest parts of our\nhumanity are laid bare, when we can finally indulge in what we spend\nmost of our time hiding away.</p>\n<p>There is another popular theory of what makes something funny, called\n<em><strong>Incongruity Theory.</strong></em> This theory posits that\nhumor is humorous because it contains two incrongruous elements, where\none element is socially normal and the other constitutes a violation of\nthe ‘subjective moral order’ [5, 6, 8]. This can be exemplified by the\nfollowing joke: a child says to her mother, “Mommy, Mommy! What is a\ndelinquent child?” and the mother replies, “Shut up and pass me the\ncrowbar” [5]. There are many shared gripes regarding the theory, mainly\nthat there is no agreed definition for the term <em>incongruity</em> and\nthat the theory isn’t comprehensive enough to explain all cases of\nlaughter [6,8].</p>\n<p><strong>3.2 Audience Laughter</strong></p>\n<p>There are two types of audience laughter in response to comedic\nmedia: <em>controlled</em> and <em>uncontrolled</em> laughter. You will\nnot find these definitions anywhere else, because I made them up.\n<em><strong>Controlled audience laughter</strong></em> occurs when the\naudience is cued to laugh, while <em><strong>uncontrolled audience\nlaughter</strong></em> occurs when the audience is free to laugh as much\nor as little (much to the dismay of standup comics) as they want. A\nclassic example of controlled laughter is laugh tracks in older sitcoms:\nattempts to stimulate contagious laughter in viewers [1, 8]. The use of\nthis device in television can be seen as an attempt to close down\nalternative readings of the content, suggesting that if you’re not\nlaughing with the track, you’re the only one [8]. In contrast, the\naudiences in standup comedy shows control how funny the act is, and\nlaugh out of genuine amusement rather than social obligation. In both\ncases, however, the audience takes on the role of a single entity,\nlaughing together or not at all. Funnily enough, the contrasting types\nof audience laughter reflect the contrast between our fake and real\nselves. In canned laugh tracks, the jokes feels disingenuous and forced\nwhile the true funniest moments are the naturally occuring unexpected\nones. <strong>Adam Conover</strong>, a standup comedian on\n<em>CollegeHumor,</em> remarked on his podcast that the biggest laugh he\nreceived on stage was in response to a comment he made on an audience\nmember’s eccentric behavior, in the moment [9].</p>\n<p><strong>4. Laughter and Death: It’s Kinda Funny</strong></p>\n<p>Laughter and Death seem like they couldn’t have less in common. Like\nlight and dark, white and black, they appear as polar opposites.\nHowever, like yin and yang, opposites attract. Laughing while processing\ndeath is actually quite common among mourners, which actually makes\nsense considering how much more tempting it is to laugh when the setting\nis inappropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>4.1 Laughter during bereavement</strong></p>\n<p>The surprising abundance of laughter during grief implies that\nlaughter may potentially serve a salutary function in the early months\nafter a loss [10]. Though at odds with traditional bereavement theories\nthat emphasize the importance of working through the emotional pain of a\nloss, <em>Duchenne laughter</em> predicts improved long-term functioning\nduring bereavement [8, 10]. As mentioned before, laughter can facilitate\na release of pent up repressed emotions that may very well relate to\ncomplex emotions around the loss of a loved one [5].</p>\n<p><strong>4.2 Inappropriate Laughter</strong></p>\n<p>Laughter at a funeral isn’t the only time laughter can feel out of\nplace. Laughter can be used in a variety of contexts, and not always for\nbeneficial reasons. Consider laughter that ostracizes others or laughter\nassociated with mental illness [5]. Bipolar disorder patients have a\ngreater propensity to initiate and enjoy humor during manic episodes\n[5]. Laughter can cut deep like a knife when it's directed against you,\nor make you look insane to others when displayed in a strange way. Both\nof these serve to illustrate just how powerful this tool is in our\nsociety, and how vulnerable it makes us. In one way or another, we live\nat the mercy of laughter, whether it’s directed at us or whether we want\nto hide it.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Discussion</strong></p>\n<p>In this paper, we discussed the history of laughter, laughter and\ncomedy, and laughter and death, as well as how different areas of\nlaughter relate to our true and fake selves. The purpose of this piece\nwas to synthesize the existing research and literature on laughter and\nits complicated relationship with humans and human nature itself.\nUltimately, I hope this paper serves as a reminder to laugh more freely.\nThe next time you enter a laughing fit, let the tears flow, contort your\nface, double over, and pay no attention to the bizarre sounds leaving\nyour mouth. Laughter shakes us out of our deadness ; When you are in\ntouch with your true self, you feel more alive, present, and embodied\n[2]. We should all embrace the animalistic, spontaneous, less refined\nside of ourselves, <em>our true selves</em>. Only then can we <em>become\nwho we are.</em></p>\n<p><strong>R<mark>eferences</mark></strong></p>\n<p>[1] R. Provine, \"Laughter: A Scientific Investigation,\" 1st ed. New\nYork, NY, USA: Penguin Books, 2000.</p>\n<p>[2] N. Alsadir, \"Animal Joy,\" 1st ed. Minneapolis, MN, USA: Coffee\nHouse Press, 2019.</p>\n<p>[3] G. Greengross and R. Mankoff, \"Inside 'Inside Jokes': The hidden\nside of humor. A review of Matthew M. Hurley, Daniel C. Dennett, and\nReginald B. Adams Jr., Inside jokes: Using humor to reverse-engineer the\nmind,\" Evolutionary Psychology: An International Journal of Evolutionary\nApproaches to Psychology and Behavior, vol. 10, pp. 443-456, Jan.\n2012.</p>\n<p>[4] P. K. Whalen, \"The emotional and physiological structure of\nlaughter: A comparison of three kinds of laughs (Antiphonal, Duchenne,\nand Voiced) and individual differences in the use of laughter in\nmiddle-aged and older marriages,\" UC Berkeley, ProQuest ID:\nWhalen_berkeley_0028E_10301. Merritt ID: ark:/13030/m5kh0s7g. Retrieved\nfrom: <a\nhref=\"https://escholarship.org/uc/item/4dv197h9\"><u>https://escholarship.org/uc/item/4dv197h9</u></a>.</p>\n<p>[5] J. Polimeni and J. Reiss, \"The First Joke: Exploring the\nEvolutionary Origins of Humor,\" Evolutionary Psychology, vol. 4, pp.\n1-29, Jan. 2006. doi: 10.1177/147470490600400129.</p>\n<p>[6] D. Keltner and G. Bonanno, \"A study of laughter and dissociation:\nDistinct correlates of laughter and smiling during bereavement,\" Journal\nof Personality and Social Psychology, vol. 73, no. 4, pp. 687-702, Nov.\n1997. doi: 10.1037/0022-3514.73.4.687.</p>\n<p>[7] K. Krys, K. Hansen, C. Xing, P. Szarota, and M. Yang, \"Do Only\nFools Smile at Strangers? Cultural Differences in Social Perception of\nIntelligence of Smiling Individuals,\" Journal of Cross-Cultural\nPsychology, vol. 45, no. 2, pp. 314-321, Feb. 2014. doi:\n10.1177/0022022113513922.</p>\n<p>[8] H. Baber, J. Donnelly, and J. Morreall, \"Taking Laughter\nSeriously,\" Philosophy and Phenomenological Research, vol. 45, no. 2,\npp. 290-303, Dec. 1984. doi: 10.2307/2107432.</p>\n<p>[9] A. Conover, \"Why Do We Laugh? with Nuar Alsadir,\" Factually,\nPodcast, March 15, 2023. [Online]. Available: <a\nhref=\"https://www.podchaser.com/podcasts/factually-with-adam-conover-853712/episodes/why-do-we-laugh-with-nuar-alsa-166704135\"><u>https://www.podchaser.com/podcasts/factually-with-adam-conover-853712/episodes/why-do-we-laugh-with-nuar-alsa-166704135</u></a>.\n[Accessed: June 6, 2023].</p>\n<p>[10] G. Bonanno, \"Laughter during bereavement,\" Bereavement Care,\nvol. 18, no. 2, pp. 19-22, 1999. doi: 10.1080/02682629908657458.</p>\n"}, {"id": "paper-five", "sourceTitle": "laughter !", "text": "Seriously Unserious\r\nWhy couldn’t the T-rex touch its toes? Because dinosaurs are extinct.\r\nChances are, you didn’t laugh at that. But I’m not alone in failing at this endeavor. Everywhere, everyday, standup comedians are bombing on stage. They are confronted by a sea of straight faces and deafening silence as they nervously chuckle and adjust their mic. Second hand embarrassment is second nature when a joke falls flat, but why do we feel this way? Why do we take the unserious so seriously?\r\nHumor is deeply intertwined with human nature. No culture exists that is unfamiliar with laughter. It’s everywhere; we use it as social lubricant, entertainment, personal expression, and to cope. Given its ubiquity, it’s easy to see why the funniest among us tend to succeed. Making your interviewer laugh is more likely to get you a job offer and even teachers have a grudging respect for their most troublesome class clown. However, given how much of an asset humor can be in daily life, there are just as many circumstances where it can backfire.\r\nI’m pretty sure my Residence Director still doesn’t like me from the time he told the staff that he was surprised none of us listened to EDM and I said that people that listen to EDM are always weird. I found out later that he likes EDM. Sometimes the unserious can have serious consequences. Staff meetings aren’t the only place where I have to curb my enthusiasm. There are too many settings where it’s inappropriate to laugh: funerals, sermons, exams, lectures, museums, presentations, in the middle of someone confessing their feelings to you. That last one is understandable, but the point still stands.\r\nNo one wants to deal with the awkwardness of a misplaced joke. Best case, it’s excruciating silence and worst case, you somehow get stuck with more duty shifts than your coworkers. It’s no wonder many of us shy away from our more comical side. In some places, laughter isn’t celebrated culturally. Even just in America, people tend to cover their mouth when laughing. In Russia, smiling is a sign of stupidity. In Norway, if a stranger smiles at you, it’s safe to assume they’re drunk, insane, American, or all of the above. Giddy has become synonymous with foolish. \r\nTo become the pinnacle of social acceptability in such cultures, we should eradicate all traces of humor. From now on, the only laughter allowed is a polite chuckle after someone offers you a well-mannered remark. Only smile when smiled at. And of course, never ever make a joke, especially not when it involves taking a risk. Obviously, this is ridiculous. Having so many social customs in place that bar human expression is not only dystopian, but widely impractical and frankly nonsensical. \r\nI have a theory that there is actually no such thing as a misplaced joke. If someone cracks a joke at a funeral, it usually lightens the mood instead of bringing it down. Including humor in a lecture makes your content more engaging, and laughing about how hard an exam was with your classmates can make you feel more at ease. Maybe still don’t laugh at the person you’re rejecting though. Jokes will never land 100% of the time. As so many standup comedians know, bombing is a part of the process. Clowns are funny (to some) because they make a fool of themselves before anyone else can. Laughing at yourself is the funniest thing you can do. \r\nTake inspiration from comedians that get back on stage after bombing. The discomfort of awkwardness is worth it to challenge yourself and spark human connection. Of course, this is much easier said than done. To be funny is to take risks, and risks are, well, risky. But if you’re serious about being funny, you’re never going to get there unless you dive in headfirst and just do it. Personally, I had this revelation last year. I used to be an extremely timid and soft spoken person when I first came to college. It was only after taking a required class, Business and Professional Speaking, where I was forced to push myself out of my comfort zone. Making a fool of yourself in front of the class was literally part of the participation grade. I remember feeling mortified, feeling extremely shaky and nervous just saying my name in front of my peers. However at some point in the semester, I realized that I just needed to take myself less seriously and get over it. On top of that, I realized how irrational it was to be scared of my classmates. After all, they were there to do the same thing as me. After I had that mini epiphany, the class not only stopped being daunting, but it became fun. I got so comfortable being on stage and speaking in front of other people that I view other class presentations in a completely different light now. Not only that, but I don’t mind making a fool of myself in front of a lot of other people because I know that it doesn’t matter at all in the long run.\r\nNot to get morbid, but life is short. Death is an inevitability for everyone everywhere. If you really think about it, nothing you do will really matter. The human race is quite literally a blip in the timeline. We only have one life to live, so why not live it to the fullest? Why not try to spark as much connection and joy while we’re here, and laugh as much as possible? Life is way too short to stop yourself from trying new things for the simple reason of not wanting to look like a fool. Being foolish and  laughing at foolish things is one of the best things life has to offer.\r\nLaughter is ingrained in our DNA. We’ve been laughing since we were monkeys. Laughing behavior can be observed in all sorts of primates– orangutans, chimpanzees, barbary macaques, etc. in Humans, laughter came before jokes, not the other way around. We’ve been laughing and tickling each other since before we had language, and then we developed humor and comedy for the sole purpose of making each other laugh more. Why shy away from the gift of our ancestors and not immerse yourself in silliness?\r\nImagine a group of friends, say Alice and her tea party from Alice in Wonderland. Imagine that, while they all share tea, The Mad Hatter pipes up and tells everyone about a cow he’s seen that can walk on two legs. Naturally, everyone is intrigued. The Cheshire Cat intrudes, asking when and where the hatter could’ve seen such a thing. With a huge grin on his face, the man with the face of Johnny Depp leans forward and yells, “At your mother’s house!” Immediately everyone at the table is overcome with bouts of sound emanating from their bellies over and over again. Except the Cheshire cat, whose smile has disappeared off his face. As soon as the hatter feels himself calming down, it only takes one look at the rest of his howling table mates to get him going again, tears emerging in his eyes. I guarantee that this moment will be forever remembered in the books. People love laughing. Not only that, it’s utterly contagious, making it that much more enjoyable when you’re laughing with your friends. \r\nThere are other contagious human behaviors. Yawning and vomiting can also incite similar expulsions from our peers. However, and this might be a reach, laughter is the most pleasant out of the three, though puking with my buddies would certainly be a bonding experience. Laughter is such a powerful force by contagion that it can bring whole communities together, as evidenced by the Tanzanian Laughing epidemic of 1962. After a few schoolchildren started laughing at a joke, the laughter quickly spread to the rest of the country, resulting in over 2000 adults laughing and crying over a period of two months. Clearly, laughter is no joke.\r\n        Laughter comes to us naturally. Not only that, it reminds us that life is meant to be enjoyed. It helps us stay grounded and enjoy the simpler things life has to offer. Children are the happiest among us because they can laugh and laugh and laugh about things we tend to take for granted. They never take themselves seriously and can laugh about anything anywhere, sometimes much to their parents’ chagrin. But we should honestly take a page out of their book. If people took themselves less seriously, there would be no need to let one’s ego get in the way of having fun or experience a bonding moment. I go back to my point of misplaced jokes being nonexistent. In such cases, someone’s offended reaction is usually more out of place than the joke itself. \r\n        Let’s revisit Wonderland. Imagine the Red Queen’s royal pet bandersnatch has just passed, and the whole kingdom has been invited to the funeral, including Alice and her cohort. Upon attending, the Hatter, feeling too grim for his liking, remarks: “Oh ho ho! I hope my funeral would be this grand. My Queen! Off with my head next!” Alice quickly shushes him, fearing her royal court over hearing. However, it’s too late, and a guard approaches them. She holds breath while the Hatter grins, but then the guard says “I know right? We lost over a hundred of our men in battle and she didn’t even bat an eye.” Upon hearing that, the Hatter entirely loses his composure. The idea that the queen’s own royal court was less respected than her pet is too much for him. Alice and the guard can’t help but laugh as well, drawing much attention to themselves against the quiet background. The Queen takes notice and is not amused, immediately thundering “who dares laugh after my baby has passed? Off with their heads!” which only makes the trio laugh more. \r\n        The joke was funny, and the queen was butthurt. You can only execute so many people before you have to take a joke at your expense. In fact, the more you try to dodge such jokes, the more people tend to make jokes about how you can’t take a joke. Even with all the power in the world, you must be able to laugh at yourself to enjoy life. Had the queen just laughed along with them, not only would she have been in on the joke, but the whole funeral’s spirits would have been lifted. Barring humor from certain situations limits human connection and opportunities to take a step back from sadness.\r\n        Growing up with a brother, I learned this lesson early in life. Having a lot of jokes made at my expense, I learned very quickly that getting upset and angry would only egg him on more. Instead, I would 'win’ our arguments by simply laughing hysterically every time he jabbed me, which frustrated him to no end. When you can laugh at yourself, no one else can laugh at you because you can just laugh with them. Not only that, but it emboldens you to try new things and step out of your comfort zone, because you no longer need to fear making a fool out of yourself. \r\n        Jokes, humour and laughter will always be a part of us. We knew how to laugh before we even knew how to speak. Babies often learn laughter before their first word. Laughing together is an integral part of human connection, and making someone else laugh is a special bond that is outdone by few other things. Most times when asked what they’re looking for in a partner, people will say someone who makes them laugh. It makes sense. Why wouldn’t you want to be with someone who makes you experience the purest most authentic expression of joy?\r\n        Tell the joke. Laugh too loud. Risk being the only person in the room who thinks you’re funny. You’ll live. Just check whether your Residence Director likes EDM before you test that theory.\n", "html": "<p><strong>Seriously Unserious</strong></p>\n<p>Why couldn’t the T-rex touch its toes? Because dinosaurs are\nextinct.</p>\n<p>Chances are, you didn’t laugh at that. But I’m not alone in failing\nat this endeavor. Everywhere, everyday, standup comedians are bombing on\nstage. They are confronted by a sea of straight faces and deafening\nsilence as they nervously chuckle and adjust their mic. Second hand\nembarrassment is second nature when a joke falls flat, but why do we\nfeel this way? Why do we take the unserious so seriously?</p>\n<p>Humor is deeply intertwined with human nature. No culture exists that\nis unfamiliar with laughter. It’s everywhere; we use it as social\nlubricant, entertainment, personal expression, and to cope. Given its\nubiquity, it’s easy to see why the funniest among us tend to succeed.\nMaking your interviewer laugh is more likely to get you a job offer and\neven teachers have a grudging respect for their most troublesome class\nclown. However, given how much of an asset humor can be in daily life,\nthere are just as many circumstances where it can backfire.</p>\n<p>I’m pretty sure my Residence Director still doesn’t like me from the\ntime he told the staff that he was surprised none of us listened to EDM\nand I said that people that listen to EDM are always weird. I found out\nlater that he likes EDM. Sometimes the unserious can have serious\nconsequences. Staff meetings aren’t the only place where I have to curb\nmy enthusiasm. There are too many settings where it’s inappropriate to\nlaugh: funerals, sermons, exams, lectures, museums, presentations, in\nthe middle of someone confessing their feelings to you. That last one is\nunderstandable, but the point still stands.</p>\n<p>No one wants to deal with the awkwardness of a misplaced joke. Best\ncase, it’s excruciating silence and worst case, you somehow get stuck\nwith more duty shifts than your coworkers. It’s no wonder many of us shy\naway from our more comical side. In some places, laughter isn’t\ncelebrated culturally. Even just in America, people tend to cover their\nmouth when laughing. In Russia, smiling is a sign of stupidity. In\nNorway, if a stranger smiles at you, it’s safe to assume they’re drunk,\ninsane, American, or all of the above. <em>Giddy</em> has become\nsynonymous with <em>foolish</em>.</p>\n<p>To become the pinnacle of social acceptability in such cultures, we\nshould eradicate all traces of humor. From now on, the only laughter\nallowed is a polite chuckle after someone offers you a well-mannered\nremark. Only smile when smiled at. And of course, never <em>ever</em>\nmake a joke, especially not when it involves taking a risk. Obviously,\nthis is ridiculous. Having so many social customs in place that bar\nhuman expression is not only dystopian, but widely impractical and\nfrankly nonsensical.</p>\n<p>I have a theory that there is actually <em>no such thing</em> as a\nmisplaced joke. If someone cracks a joke at a funeral, it usually\nlightens the mood instead of bringing it down. Including humor in a\nlecture makes your content more engaging, and laughing about how hard an\nexam was with your classmates can make you feel more at ease. Maybe\nstill don’t laugh at the person you’re rejecting though. Jokes will\nnever land 100% of the time. As so many standup comedians know, bombing\nis a part of the process. Clowns are funny (to some) because they make a\nfool of themselves before anyone else can. Laughing at yourself is the\nfunniest thing you can do.</p>\n<p>Take inspiration from comedians that get back on stage after bombing.\nThe discomfort of awkwardness is worth it to challenge yourself and\nspark human connection. Of course, this is much easier said than done.\nTo be funny is to take risks, and risks are, well, <em>risky</em>. But\nif you’re serious about being funny, you’re never going to get there\nunless you dive in headfirst and just do it. Personally, I had this\nrevelation last year. I used to be an extremely timid and soft spoken\nperson when I first came to college. It was only after taking a required\nclass, Business and Professional Speaking, where I was forced to push\nmyself out of my comfort zone. Making a fool of yourself in front of the\nclass was literally part of the participation grade. I remember feeling\nmortified, feeling extremely shaky and nervous just saying my name in\nfront of my peers. However at some point in the semester, I realized\nthat I just needed to take myself less seriously and get over it. On top\nof that, I realized how irrational it was to be scared of my classmates.\nAfter all, they were there to do the same thing as me. After I had that\nmini epiphany, the class not only stopped being daunting, but it became\n<em>fun</em>. I got so comfortable being on stage and speaking in front\nof other people that I view other class presentations in a completely\ndifferent light now. Not only that, but I don’t mind making a fool of\nmyself in front of a lot of other people because I know that it doesn’t\nmatter at all in the long run.</p>\n<p>Not to get morbid, but life is short. Death is an inevitability for\neveryone everywhere. If you really think about it, nothing you do will\nreally matter. The human race is quite literally a blip in the timeline.\nWe only have one life to live, so why not live it to the fullest? Why\nnot try to spark as much connection and joy while we’re here, and laugh\nas much as possible? Life is way too short to stop yourself from trying\nnew things for the simple reason of not wanting to look like a fool.\nBeing foolish and laughing at foolish things is one of the best things\nlife has to offer.</p>\n<p>Laughter is ingrained in our DNA. We’ve been laughing since we were\nmonkeys. Laughing behavior can be observed in all sorts of primates–\norangutans, chimpanzees, barbary macaques, etc. in Humans, laughter came\nbefore jokes, not the other way around. We’ve been laughing and tickling\neach other since before we had language, and then we developed humor and\ncomedy for the sole purpose of making each other laugh more. Why shy\naway from the gift of our ancestors and not immerse yourself in\nsilliness?</p>\n<p>Imagine a group of friends, say Alice and her tea party from Alice in\nWonderland. Imagine that, while they all share tea, The Mad Hatter pipes\nup and tells everyone about a cow he’s seen that can walk on two legs.\nNaturally, everyone is intrigued. The Cheshire Cat intrudes, asking when\nand where the hatter could’ve seen such a thing. With a huge grin on his\nface, the man with the face of Johnny Depp leans forward and yells, “At\nyour mother’s house!” Immediately everyone at the table is overcome with\nbouts of sound emanating from their bellies over and over again. Except\nthe Cheshire cat, whose smile has disappeared off his face. As soon as\nthe hatter feels himself calming down, it only takes one look at the\nrest of his howling table mates to get him going again, tears emerging\nin his eyes. I guarantee that this moment will be forever remembered in\nthe books. People love laughing. Not only that, it’s utterly contagious,\nmaking it that much more enjoyable when you’re laughing with your\nfriends.</p>\n<p>There are other contagious human behaviors. Yawning and vomiting can\nalso incite similar expulsions from our peers. However, and this might\nbe a reach, laughter is the most pleasant out of the three, though\npuking with my buddies would certainly be a bonding experience. Laughter\nis such a powerful force by contagion that it can bring whole\ncommunities together, as evidenced by the Tanzanian Laughing epidemic of\n1962. After a few schoolchildren started laughing at a joke, the\nlaughter quickly spread to the rest of the country, resulting in over\n2000 adults laughing and crying over a period of two months. Clearly,\nlaughter is no joke.</p>\n<p>Laughter comes to us naturally. Not only that, it reminds us that\nlife is meant to be enjoyed. It helps us stay grounded and enjoy the\nsimpler things life has to offer. Children are the happiest among us\nbecause they can laugh and laugh and laugh about things we tend to take\nfor granted. They never take themselves seriously and can laugh about\nanything anywhere, sometimes much to their parents’ chagrin. But we\nshould honestly take a page out of their book. If people took themselves\nless seriously, there would be no need to let one’s ego get in the way\nof having fun or experience a bonding moment. I go back to my point of\nmisplaced jokes being nonexistent. In such cases, someone’s offended\nreaction is usually more out of place than the joke itself.</p>\n<p>Let’s revisit Wonderland. Imagine the Red Queen’s royal pet\nbandersnatch has just passed, and the whole kingdom has been invited to\nthe funeral, including Alice and her cohort. Upon attending, the Hatter,\nfeeling too grim for his liking, remarks: “Oh ho ho! I hope my funeral\nwould be this grand. My Queen! Off with my head next!” Alice quickly\nshushes him, fearing her royal court over hearing. However, it’s too\nlate, and a guard approaches them. She holds breath while the Hatter\ngrins, but then the guard says “I know right? We lost over a hundred of\nour men in battle and she didn’t even bat an eye.” Upon hearing that,\nthe Hatter entirely loses his composure. The idea that the queen’s own\nroyal court was less respected than her pet is too much for him. Alice\nand the guard can’t help but laugh as well, drawing much attention to\nthemselves against the quiet background. The Queen takes notice and is\nnot amused, immediately thundering “who <em>dares</em> laugh after my\nbaby has passed? Off with their heads!” which only makes the trio laugh\nmore.</p>\n<p>The joke was funny, and the queen was butthurt. You can only execute\nso many people before you have to take a joke at your expense. In fact,\nthe more you try to dodge such jokes, the more people tend to make jokes\nabout how you can’t take a joke. Even with all the power in the world,\nyou must be able to laugh at yourself to enjoy life. Had the queen just\nlaughed along with them, not only would she have been in on the joke,\nbut the whole funeral’s spirits would have been lifted. Barring humor\nfrom certain situations limits human connection and opportunities to\ntake a step back from sadness.</p>\n<p>Growing up with a brother, I learned this lesson early in life.\nHaving a lot of jokes made at my expense, I learned very quickly that\ngetting upset and angry would only egg him on more. Instead, I would\n'win’ our arguments by simply laughing hysterically every time he jabbed\nme, which frustrated him to no end. When you can laugh at yourself, no\none else can laugh at you because you can just laugh with them. Not only\nthat, but it emboldens you to try new things and step out of your\ncomfort zone, because you no longer need to fear making a fool out of\nyourself.</p>\n<p>Jokes, humour and laughter will always be a part of us. We knew how\nto laugh before we even knew how to speak. Babies often learn laughter\nbefore their first word. Laughing together is an integral part of human\nconnection, and making someone else laugh is a special bond that is\noutdone by few other things. Most times when asked what they’re looking\nfor in a partner, people will say someone who makes them laugh. It makes\nsense. Why wouldn’t you want to be with someone who makes you experience\nthe purest most authentic expression of joy?</p>\n<p>Tell the joke. Laugh too loud. Risk being the only person in the room who thinks you’re funny. You’ll live. Just check whether your Residence Director likes EDM before you test that theory.</p>\n"}, {"id": "paper-six", "sourceTitle": "Voqal Scoping Review", "text": "Examining Limits of Psychiatric Care Infrastructure Through a South Asian Lens\r\nNalini Kantheti\r\n\r\n\r\nAbstract: Mental health crises are an inevitable part of human society, yet systems designed to respond to severe mental illness have often prioritized crisis management and social control over continuous, patient-centered care. This literature review examines the historical development of psychiatric infrastructure, the systemic gaps experienced by people with chronic severe mental illness, patterns of recurrent hospitalization and crisis response, and how these issues are experienced within South Asian families. By synthesizing these bodies of literature, this review identifies limitations in existing psychiatric care systems and establishes a foundation for considering more continuous, culturally responsive, and community-based interventions.\r\n\r\n\r\n1. Introduction\r\nMental illness has long been an unavoidable part of human society. Yet, on institutional, societal, and interpersonal levels, mentally ill people are too often treated as a problem to solve rather than a population to serve. Mental illness is often treated as an individual problem, but this framing can obscure the systemic failures that shape patients’ experiences of care. This is called medicalization, which is the “dominant medical perspective [that] suggests that the problem is individual rather than social in scope.” The problem with this model is that it’s “mainly acute and does not deal appropriately with chronic conditions.”[1] A better alternative approach to designing psychiatric care infrastructure prioritizes community care, but “[integrating] multiple cultural, social and economic aspects is a much more challenging approach than [analysing] contributing factors on an individual level.”[3] \r\nHowever, this paper does take cultural aspects into account when discussing these systems and their limitations by using a South Asian lens to do so. This choice was made because “South Asians (SAs) underutilize mental health services compared to many racial and ethnic groups in the United States (US),” and “little attention has been given specifically to service utilization among South Asians who originate from Bangladesh, Bhutan, India, the Maldives, Nepal, Pakistan, and Sri Lanka.” [4, 5] Not only are South Asians underrepresented in psychiatric literature, but they drastically underuse mental health services due to lack of education and cultural norms. [5]\r\nThe purpose of this literature review is to examine limitations of current psychiatric care infrastructure through the South Asian lens and establish a foundation for considering small-scale interventions that could mitigate the consequences of those limitations. To do this I will start by tracing the history of psychiatric institutions and explore the historical movement from institutionalization to community care. Then, I will discuss the phenomenon of frequent recurrent hospitalization that happens as a result of prioritizing stabilizing acute crises rather than sustained continuation of care, and the role police involvement plays in this process. Next, I will identify and relate limitations in psychiatric care infrastructure to South Asian family experiences, and finally, use the existing literature to derive principles for more culturally responsive and community-oriented care.\r\n\r\n\r\n2. Origin and Evolution of Psychiatric Institutions\r\n        In order to better understand the current state of psychiatric care, it is vital to first understand the two major models that have shaped its development: institutionalization and  deinstitutionalization. These processes describe how initial societal responses to severe mental illness manifested as the asylum, and then how severe problems with these institutions later catalysed a shift to more community oriented care. This process is known as deinstitutionalization, and it also came with its own plethora of problems. The history of psychiatric care is therefore not a straightforward progression from a harmful model to a better one. Institutionalization and deinstitutionalization each addressed different needs while producing different limitations. \r\n2.1 Institutionalization\r\n        Psychiatric institutions emerged as one of many attempts to manage and treat severe mental illness. Historically, responses to mental illness included “psychosurgery, mechanical contention, institutionalization, ECT, and prescription of medication.” [2] Within this broader history, the asylum became a dominant model of care because it concentrated treatment, supervision, housing, and daily structure within one institution.\r\n        In theory, this arrangement provided something that modern psychiatric systems struggle to provide: continuity of care. A patient living within an institution was not repeatedly transferred between separate systems for housing, crisis response, treatment, and supervision but instead experienced one system that managed all these aspects at once. That being said, such continuity came at an expensive cost.\r\n        Abysmal conditions including isolation, overcrowding, physical disease, loss of autonomy, and coercion soon became synonymous with asylum rather than the idea of a safe haven the name promised. [2]  In many instances, it was “an inhospitable territory of human misery and affliction.” [2] The institution itself contributed to a patient’s deterioration on top of their admission-causing illness, often “resulting in a condition that was ‘characterized by apathy, lack of initiative, loss of interest and submissiveness’. ”[2] In this way, institutionalization could paradoxically create behaviors that made patients appear even more dependent on institutional care. This growing concern over confinement, autonomy, dignity, and psychiatric power became a major force behind the movement away from long-term institutional care.\r\n2.2 Deinstitutionalization\r\n        Following the Second World War, “mental health care shifted from being based in residential asylums to becoming primarily an outpatient service” [2] This process was known as deinstitutionalization. Deinstitutionalization was not merely a process of transitioning care; “It undoubtedly implied a significant philosophical sea change”... [involving] measures like the improvement of conditions for patients, ending restraints, reducing electro-shock treatment, opening up the wards and knocking down walls and fences.” [2]. The goal was not simply to move patients from one location to another, but to create a system in which people with mental illness could retain greater autonomy and participate more fully in community life.\r\n        Community care theoretically would offer integration of psychiatric care with family support and housing and employment help, but because adequate community infrastructure did not consistently develop alongside deinstitutionalization, “families in many countries [were] left to fill in the cracks left by an inadequate mental health system, providing much of the care formerly supplied by the state or private hospitals.” [2] Not only this, but “it has been argued that there are now as many people in some form of incarceration as there were in the old hospitals,” a process unfortunately referred to as transinstitutionalization. \r\n\r\n\r\n3. A System Oriented Toward Crisis Rather Than Continuity\r\n        The central historical tension we now must reconcile with is illustrated as follows. Institutionalization provided continuity but frequently sacrificed autonomy. Deinstitutionalization increased autonomy but often failed to create adequate continuity. This tension is reflected in the modern psychiatric system, specifically in the way it prioritizes stabilizing acute crises rather than continuity of care. \r\n3.1 The Revolving Door Phenomenon\r\n        One consequence of this system is the phenomenon of patients experiencing frequent recurrent hospitalizations that seem to be stuck in an equilibrium between crisis and reintegration. This is known as the revolving door phenomenon, and is clinically defined in two types: “Type 1): at least four admissions in a 10-year period, with no admission or discharge period lasting more than 2.5 years; or (Type 2): at least four admissions during the first 2.5 years after first admission.” [3].\r\n        It’s quite important to note that “This phenomenon started to occur after the deinstitutionalization policies … in the second half of the 20th century.” [3] This is because “The emphasis on deinstitutionalization has increased the risk of frequent readmissions in many patients, now treated in the community.” [3]\r\nWe don’t necessarily know exactly what causes this phenomenon, but a “lack of social integration may contribute to relapse.“ [3] Additionally, it is speculated that “the repeated use of inpatient care, which can serve for some patients as a shelter from adverse life conditions, could lead to a sort of dependence on the service itself and create a vicious cycle of repetitive admissions.” [3]\r\n3.2 Police Involvement in Mental Crises\r\n        It’s also important to acknowledge the role law enforcement plays in the system, as it can be an unavoidable part of many patients’ journeys when experiencing repeat hospitalizations. I say this because ”contemporary health care systems, social programs, and policing models have been designed in such a way that contact between people with mental disorders and the police is inevitable” [6] “one in ten individuals [encounter] police in their pathway to mental health care,” and  “25% of persons with mental disorders have been arrested by police at some point in their lifetime.” [6]\r\n        Not only is police contact a recurring component of many patients’ interactions with navigating mental health infrastructure, but in many cases the consequences of such interactions can be severe, resulting in tragically avoidable deaths. “Overall, 203 of 633 fatal police encounters (32%) involved victims who showed signs of an MHC at the time of their death.” [7] These findings do not suggest that every police encounter involving mental illness is violent, but they do demonstrate the risks of relying on law-enforcement systems as a routine component of psychiatric crisis response.\r\n \r\n4. A South Asian Perspective\r\n        Taken together, recurrent hospitalization and police involvement suggest that post-deinstitutionalization psychiatric care is not simply inadequate, but fragmented across hospitals, families, community services, and law enforcement. Additionally, the effects of that fragmentation are not experienced uniformly. They are mediated by culture, family structure, stigma, language, and access to care, making a South Asian lens particularly useful for examining how systemic limitations are compounded in practice.\r\n        Why focus on South Asians? South Asians underutilize mental-health services relative to many U.S. racial/ethnic groups. Additionally, cultural expectations surrounding educational and occupational success, marriage, family reputation, and appropriate behavior can also shape how psychiatric symptoms are interpreted and responded to. Mental illness may therefore affect not only the individual, but also the social roles and expectations surrounding them, making the family an especially important part of understanding how psychiatric care is navigated.\r\n4.1 The Family as a Unit\r\nFor many South Asian patients, the family is not simply a source of emotional support but an active part of psychiatric care. Family members may be the first to recognize behavioral changes, facilitate entry into treatment, monitor crises, make decisions, and provide support between episodes of formal care. Mohsin et al. found high levels of family involvement among South Asian patients with severe mental illness, but that involvement was not uniformly positive. Families ranged from highly supportive to disparaging, and some relatives remained involved primarily out of obligation despite strained relationships [5]. Patients could therefore be heavily surrounded by family while still feeling misunderstood, lonely, or inferior [5].\r\nThis creates an important tension between support and obligation. Family involvement can provide continuity that the formal mental-health system often lacks, but it can also introduce conflict, control, or resentment. When psychiatric care is fragmented, families may become responsible for maintaining stability between hospitalizations without necessarily having the knowledge, resources, or support required to do so.\r\n4.2 Cultural Stigma\r\nCultural stigma complicates the role of the family because the consequences of severe mental illness often extend beyond the diagnosed individual. In the South Asian context, mental illness can become a social condition experienced by the entire household. Koschorke et al. found that caregivers feared not only that the person with schizophrenia would be judged or disrespected, but that stigma would spread outward to other family members through blame, avoidance, and gossip [4]. Caregivers described anxiety that others would “spread the news,” avoid the family, or interpret the presence of mental illness as evidence of failure within the household itself [4].\r\nThis pressure could alter how families interacted with their communities. Some caregivers restricted the affected family member’s contact with others, avoided social events, or withdrew from neighbors and relatives because they feared uncomfortable questions or negative reactions [4]. In other cases, that withdrawal was imposed from the outside: caregivers described friends, neighbors, and acquaintances who stopped visiting, stopped speaking to them, or excluded them from social functions after learning about the illness [4]. The stigma attached to severe mental illness therefore did not remain confined to the patient. Caregivers themselves could lose social standing simply because of their relationship to the person who was ill.\r\nThe criticism directed toward caregivers was often intensely personal. Families reported being blamed for having “made” the person mentally ill, for failing to control their behavior, for not caring for them properly, or for failing to ensure that they worked or married [4]. One caregiver recalled hearing others say that her husband had been “roaming around as mad” because she was “not taking care of him.” She explained simply: “When they speak like this, I feel very bad” [4]. Another stated, “They criticise me. They criticise me even now for his behaviour” [4]. These accounts show how responsibility for psychiatric illness can be displaced onto the family, even when caregivers themselves are already managing the practical and emotional demands of severe illness.\r\nThe consequences can become particularly painful when mental illness threatens expectations surrounding marriage, work, reputation, and family status. Caregivers have described being criticized for not marrying an affected relative, not making sure that they worked, or even for allowing a person with mental illness to enter the family through marriage [4]. These reactions reflect a social environment in which illness is not treated solely as a health condition, but as something capable of disrupting the expected social trajectory of an entire family.\r\nSome caregivers described this loss of status in devastating terms. One reported that the family had effectively been labeled a “mad house” [4]. Another said:\r\n“My life is over. What is there in my life now? He is not going to become normal. (…) We are not going to be respected by others.” [4]\r\nThis statement captures how deeply stigma can reshape the caregiver’s own identity. The burden is not limited to managing symptoms or helping a relative navigate treatment; it also includes the possibility of becoming socially marked through association with that illness. Severe mental illness can therefore alter not only the patient’s social position, but the perceived respectability and future of the family as a whole.\r\nPerhaps most strikingly, caregivers did not always challenge this treatment. Koschorke et al. note that relatively few expressed anger toward people who stigmatized them. Instead, some appeared to understand exclusion or criticism as a natural consequence of the affected person’s “different behaviour” [4]. This suggests that stigma can become internalized within the family itself. When social rejection is treated as expected or justified, families may begin adapting themselves to stigma rather than resisting it.\r\nThis dynamic also complicates the assumption that family involvement is inherently protective. A family may simultaneously provide housing, supervision, financial support, transportation, and crisis management while also carrying beliefs that produce shame, blame, or social isolation. The family can therefore function as both a source of continuity and a site where cultural stigma is reproduced. That contradiction is especially important in a system that already depends heavily on relatives to maintain care between episodes of formal treatment.\r\nThe result is a deeply unstable form of informal infrastructure. Families may be expected to recognize symptoms, manage crises, encourage treatment, and provide long-term support, while at the same time facing judgment from their communities and sometimes lacking a framework for understanding the illness themselves. In that context, stigma does more than create embarrassment. It can shape whether illness is acknowledged, whether care is sought, how patients are treated within the household, and whether caregivers themselves remain socially connected or become isolated.\r\nThis is why cultural stigma is not peripheral to psychiatric infrastructure. If the formal system relies on families to provide continuity, then the beliefs and pressures surrounding those families become part of the care system itself.\r\n4.3 Lack of Education\r\nStigma is also closely connected to how mental illness is understood. Koschorke et al. found relatively low levels of knowledge about schizophrenia among caregivers, with some interpreting symptoms as intentional misbehavior, laziness, or a lack of effort rather than manifestations of illness [4]. Other caregivers attributed symptoms to family conflict, poor upbringing, or insufficient affection [4]. These interpretations can shape how families respond to emerging psychiatric symptoms and may delay recognition that professional care is needed.\r\nMohsin et al. identified a similar problem among South Asian patients in New York City. Many participants reported lacking the language to describe their emotional or psychiatric experiences, and some did not know their formal diagnosis even after entering treatment [5]. The only South Asian term frequently identified for severe mental illness was ‘paagal’, meaning “crazy,” which carries a strongly derogatory meaning [5]. Participants could often recognize that something had changed in their behavior or ability to fulfill expected family roles without having the vocabulary to identify what they were experiencing [5].\r\nImportantly, increasing biomedical knowledge alone does not necessarily eliminate stigma. Koschorke et al. found that greater awareness of biomedical explanations for schizophrenia did not consistently protect families from stigma and, in some cases, was associated with greater discrimination toward patients [4]. Education may therefore be most useful when it does more than simply attach a diagnostic label to symptoms. Information about the nature and management of symptoms may reduce blame, but effective interventions must also account for culturally specific beliefs about illness, behavior, responsibility, and family roles [4].\r\n4.4 Structural and Immigration-Related Barriers\r\nCultural barriers operate alongside structural ones. Mohsin et al. found that immigration status, insurance coverage, difficulty scheduling appointments, and fragmented relationships with multiple providers could interfere with South Asian patients’ ability to access and remain engaged in care [5]. For first-generation immigrants in particular, limited knowledge about mental health and low perceived need for treatment could mean that formal psychiatric care was not accessed until symptoms had already become severe [5].\r\nImmigration concerns may also intersect directly with psychiatric crises. One participant described the pressure created when a parent’s psychiatric hospitalization occurred immediately before the family’s green-card interview, demonstrating how psychiatric instability can disrupt immigration processes that depend on documentation, attendance, and perceived stability [5]. These pressures further complicate the assumption that families can simply navigate the healthcare system once a crisis occurs.\r\nTaken together, these findings reveal a central contradiction in post-deinstitutionalization care: the formal system is episodic, while the family is continuous. Families may provide the support that fills the gaps between hospitalizations, but they may simultaneously be navigating stigma, limited mental-health knowledge, strained relationships, financial or immigration pressures, and fragmented services. Many caregivers themselves appeared isolated and unsupported because fear of stigma led them to withdraw from social interaction [4].\r\n\r\n\r\n5. Imagining a Better System\r\nThe literature reviewed thus far points to a psychiatric system that is fragmented across hospitals, families, community services, and law enforcement. For South Asian families, that fragmentation is further complicated by stigma, limited mental-health knowledge, language barriers, and immigration-related constraints. If families are already performing part of the work of psychiatric infrastructure, the central question becomes how formal systems can support them without simply transferring responsibility for chronic psychiatric care onto them.\r\n5.1 Gaps in the Literature\r\nSeveral important gaps remain. There is limited research examining what occurs between psychiatric admissions, particularly from the perspective of families providing ongoing care. Research on severe mental illness among South Asians in the United States is also sparse; Mohsin et al. note that there are no published prevalence or service-utilization data for severe depression, bipolar disorder, and schizophrenia among U.S. South Asians [5]. Research is even more limited on how South Asian families navigate recurrent severe illness over time, how immigration-related pressures influence crisis response, and how families interact with emergency or law-enforcement systems. Existing studies also often capture individual encounters rather than the full trajectory of repeated crises, hospitalization, discharge, and family caregiving.\r\n5.2 Principles for More Responsive Care\r\nThe literature nevertheless suggests several principles for improving care. First, interventions should involve the family unit, because families may function as both major sources of support and major sources of stress [5]. Second, culturally salient education should help families recognize symptoms, understand available treatment, and reduce the tendency to interpret psychiatric symptoms as intentional behavior or personal failure. However, education cannot rely on biomedical information alone; Koschorke et al. found that greater biomedical knowledge did not automatically reduce stigma, suggesting that interventions must also address culturally specific beliefs about responsibility, family reputation, and illness [4]. \r\nCare must also be linguistically and culturally accessible. Language differences, dialect mismatches, lack of insurance, immigration status, fragmented providers, and difficulty navigating appointments can all interfere with sustained treatment [5]. Earlier intervention is equally important, particularly because many South Asian patients described entering formal psychiatric care only after symptoms had become highly disruptive or when families felt they had no other option [5].\r\n5.3 Alternative Crisis-Response Infrastructure\r\nBecause police often become involved in mental-health crises that are unrelated to criminal behavior, the literature increasingly questions whether law enforcement should function as the default crisis-response system [6]. Police themselves have reported frustration with gaps in health and social services that leave them responsible for situations they are not well positioned to resolve [6]. Alternative response models that deploy mental-health professionals instead of police for eligible crisis calls, including programs such as B-HEARD and CAHOOTS, offer one approach to reducing unnecessary law-enforcement involvement and the risk of escalation [7].\r\n5.4 Designing for Care Rather Than Social Control\r\nThe literature ultimately points to a broader question about what psychiatric systems are designed to accomplish. Aviram argues that public concern about mental-health reform often intensifies only when mental illness becomes socially visible or disruptive, and that mental-health policy has historically been shaped heavily by considerations of social control [1]. This orientation is also reflected in systems where treatment decisions remain largely provider-dominated [8], and where police and emergency services become responsible for people whose needs were not adequately addressed earlier. \r\nA care-oriented system would instead prioritize continuity, patient participation, and sustained community support. Maples argues that patients should play an active role in directing their own care, while Aviram emphasizes the need for a comprehensive community system supported by organizational change, advocacy, and greater investment in medical and social services [1], [8]. Importantly, community care should not simply mean transferring unpaid responsibility from institutions to families. Care must instead be distributed across coordinated health, social-service, family, and community systems.\r\nThis also requires moving beyond episodic stabilization. People with severe mental illness may need ongoing support with employment, benefits, housing, treatment, and reintegration into community life [1], [3]. Improving outcomes therefore requires simultaneous changes in both healthcare and the broader social environment, including better access, communication across agencies, patient education, and support before crises become severe [8]. The goal should not simply be to contain crises, but to create conditions in which fewer crises occur in the first place.\r\n\r\n\r\n6. Discussion\r\nAcross the literature, a consistent cycle emerges. Psychiatric institutions once concentrated responsibility for people with severe mental illness, but deinstitutionalization redistributed that responsibility across hospitals, community services, families, and emergency systems without always creating adequate continuity of care. As a result, some patients move repeatedly through crisis, hospitalization, stabilization, discharge, family or community management, deterioration, emergency or police intervention, and hospitalization again. South Asian families may experience this cycle with additional pressures related to stigma, limited mental-health knowledge, cultural expectations, language barriers, immigration concerns, and strained family relationships. These findings reveal several recurring tensions: community care can become burden shifting, family support can coexist with family stigma, greater knowledge does not automatically eliminate stigma, and systems intended to provide care can also function as mechanisms of social control.\r\nA major limitation of the existing literature is that these experiences are often studied separately. Research on hospitalization focuses on readmission, policing research focuses on crisis encounters, stigma research focuses on families, and community-care research focuses on service delivery. Yet patients and families may experience all of these stages as one continuous process. In this sense, the literature fragments a process that families experience continuously. Severe mental illness therefore cannot be understood solely at the level of the individual patient; it must also be understood through the systems and relationships that shape what happens before, during, and after crisis. Ultimately, the measure of a psychiatric care system should not be whether it can contain a crisis, but whether it can sustain a person beyond one. At the center of that goal remains a simple principle identified by participants in Mohsin et al.: “what mattered most was being treated well.” [5].\r\n\r\n\r\n________________\r\n\r\n\r\nReferences\r\n[1] U. Aviram, “Community care of the seriously mentally ill: Continuing problems and current issues,” Community Mental Health Journal, vol. 26, no. 1, pp. 69–88, Feb. 1990, doi: 10.1007/BF00752677. PubMed\r\n[2] F. Balbuena Rivera, “From asylums to deinstitutionalization and after: An analytic review,” International Journal of Social Psychiatry, vol. 70, no. 8, pp. 1392–1401, Dec. 2024, doi: 10.1177/00207640241268356. PubMed\r\n[3] J. Fonseca Barbosa and J. Gama Marques, “The revolving door phenomenon in severe psychiatric disorders: A systematic review,” International Journal of Social Psychiatry, vol. 69, no. 5, pp. 1075–1089, Aug. 2023, doi: 10.1177/00207640221143282. PubMed\r\n[4] M. Koschorke et al., “Experiences of stigma and discrimination faced by family caregivers of people with schizophrenia in India,” Social Science & Medicine, vol. 178, pp. 66–77, Apr. 2017, doi: 10.1016/j.socscimed.2017.01.061. PubMed\r\n[5] F. Mohsin, S. Aravala, T. Rahman, S. H. Ali, M. D. Taher, P. Mitra, and S. Misra, “Psychiatric healthcare experiences of South Asian patients with severe mental illness diagnoses and their families in New York City: A qualitative study,” Community Mental Health Journal, vol. 61, no. 1, pp. 39–49, Jan. 2025, doi: 10.1007/s10597-024-01323-1. PubMed Central (PMC)\r\n[6] J. D. Livingston, “Contact between police and people with mental disorders: A review of rates,” Psychiatric Services, vol. 67, no. 8, pp. 850–857, Aug. 2016, doi: 10.1176/appi.ps.201500312. PubMed\r\n[7] H. Khan, M. Miller, C. Barber, and D. Azrael, “Fatal police shootings of victims with mental health crises: A descriptive analysis of data from the 2014–2015 National Violent Death Reporting System,” Journal of Urban Health, vol. 101, no. 2, pp. 262–271, Apr. 2024, doi: 10.1007/s11524-024-00833-3. PubMed\r\n[8] N. J. Maples, “Shared decision making in the care of post-acute psychiatric individuals with serious mental illness,” Dr.P.H. dissertation, School of Public Health, The University of Texas Health Science Center at Houston, Houston, TX, USA, 2020.", "html": "<p><strong>Examining Limits of Psychiatric Care Infrastructure Through a\nSouth Asian Lens</strong></p>\n<p>Nalini Kantheti</p>\n<p><em><strong>Abstract:</strong> Mental health crises are an inevitable\npart of human society, yet systems designed to respond to severe mental\nillness have often prioritized crisis management and social control over\ncontinuous, patient-centered care. This literature review examines the\nhistorical development of psychiatric infrastructure, the systemic gaps\nexperienced by people with chronic severe mental illness, patterns of\nrecurrent hospitalization and crisis response, and how these issues are\nexperienced within South Asian families. By synthesizing these bodies of\nliterature, this review identifies limitations in existing psychiatric\ncare systems and establishes a foundation for considering more\ncontinuous, culturally responsive, and community-based\ninterventions.</em></p>\n<p><strong>1. Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>Mental illness has long been an unavoidable part of human society.\nYet, on institutional, societal, and interpersonal levels, mentally ill\npeople are too often treated as a problem to solve rather than a\npopulation to serve. Mental illness is often treated as an individual\nproblem, but this framing can obscure the systemic failures that shape\npatients’ experiences of care. This is called <em>medicalization,</em>\nwhich is the “dominant medical perspective [that] suggests that the\nproblem is individual rather than social in scope.” The problem with\nthis model is that it’s “mainly acute and does not deal appropriately\nwith chronic conditions.”[1] A better alternative approach to designing\npsychiatric care infrastructure prioritizes community care, but\n“[integrating] multiple cultural, social and economic aspects is a much\nmore challenging approach than [analysing] contributing factors on an\nindividual level.”[3]</p>\n<p>However, this paper does take cultural aspects into account when\ndiscussing these systems and their limitations by using a South Asian\nlens to do so. This choice was made because “South Asians (SAs)\nunderutilize mental health services compared to many racial and ethnic\ngroups in the United States (US),” and “little attention has been given\nspecifically to service utilization among South Asians who originate\nfrom Bangladesh, Bhutan, India, the Maldives, Nepal, Pakistan, and Sri\nLanka.” [4, 5] Not only are South Asians underrepresented in psychiatric\nliterature, but they drastically underuse mental health services due to\nlack of education and cultural norms. [5]</p>\n<p>The purpose of this literature review is to examine limitations of\ncurrent psychiatric care infrastructure through the South Asian lens and\nestablish a foundation for considering small-scale interventions that\ncould mitigate the consequences of those limitations. To do this I will\nstart by tracing the history of psychiatric institutions and explore the\nhistorical movement from institutionalization to community care. Then, I\nwill discuss the phenomenon of frequent recurrent hospitalization that\nhappens as a result of prioritizing stabilizing acute crises rather than\nsustained continuation of care, and the role police involvement plays in\nthis process. Next, I will identify and relate limitations in\npsychiatric care infrastructure to South Asian family experiences, and\nfinally, use the existing literature to derive principles for more\nculturally responsive and community-oriented care.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Origin and Evolution of Psychiatric\nInstitutions</strong></p>\n<p>In order to better understand the current state of psychiatric care,\nit is vital to first understand the two major models that have shaped\nits development: <em>institutionalization</em> and\n<em>deinstitutionalization</em>. These processes describe how initial\nsocietal responses to severe mental illness manifested as the\n<strong>asylum</strong>, and then how severe problems with these\ninstitutions later catalysed a shift to more <strong>community oriented\ncare</strong>. This process is known as <em>deinstitutionalization</em>,\nand it also came with its own plethora of problems. The history of\npsychiatric care is therefore not a straightforward progression from a\nharmful model to a better one. Institutionalization and\ndeinstitutionalization each addressed different needs while producing\ndifferent limitations.</p>\n<p><strong>2.1 Institutionalization</strong></p>\n<p>Psychiatric institutions emerged as one of many attempts to manage\nand treat severe mental illness. Historically, responses to mental\nillness included “psychosurgery, mechanical contention,\ninstitutionalization, ECT, and prescription of medication.” [2] Within\nthis broader history, the asylum became a dominant model of care because\nit concentrated treatment, supervision, housing, and daily structure\nwithin one institution.</p>\n<p>In theory, this arrangement provided something that modern\npsychiatric systems struggle to provide: continuity of care. A patient\nliving within an institution was not repeatedly transferred between\nseparate systems for housing, crisis response, treatment, and\nsupervision but instead experienced one system that managed all these\naspects at once. That being said, such continuity came at an expensive\ncost.</p>\n<p>Abysmal conditions including isolation, overcrowding, physical\ndisease, loss of autonomy, and coercion soon became synonymous with\nasylum rather than the idea of a safe haven the name promised. [2] In\nmany instances, it was “an inhospitable territory of human misery and\naffliction.” [2] The institution itself contributed to a patient’s\ndeterioration on top of their admission-causing illness, often\n“resulting in a condition that was ‘characterized by apathy, lack of\ninitiative, loss of interest and submissiveness’. ”[2] In this way,\ninstitutionalization could paradoxically create behaviors that made\npatients appear even more dependent on institutional care. This growing\nconcern over confinement, autonomy, dignity, and psychiatric power\nbecame a major force behind the movement away from long-term\ninstitutional care.</p>\n<p><strong>2.2 Deinstitutionalization</strong></p>\n<p>Following the Second World War<em>, “</em>mental health care shifted\nfrom being based in residential asylums to becoming primarily an\noutpatient service” [2] This process was known as\n<em>deinstitutionalization.</em> Deinstitutionalization was not merely a\nprocess of transitioning care; “It undoubtedly implied a significant\nphilosophical sea change”... [involving] measures like the improvement\nof conditions for patients, ending restraints, reducing electro-shock\ntreatment, opening up the wards and knocking down walls and fences.”\n[2]. The goal was not simply to move patients from one location to\nanother, but to create a system in which people with mental illness\ncould retain greater autonomy and participate more fully in community\nlife.</p>\n<p>Community care theoretically would offer integration of psychiatric\ncare with family support and housing and employment help, but because\nadequate community infrastructure did not consistently develop alongside\ndeinstitutionalization, “families in many countries [were] left to fill\nin the cracks left by an inadequate mental health system, providing much\nof the care formerly supplied by the state or private hospitals.” [2]\nNot only this, but “it has been argued that there are now as many people\nin some form of incarceration as there were in the old hospitals,” a\nprocess unfortunately referred to as\n<em>transinstitutionalization.</em></p>\n<p><strong>3. A System Oriented Toward Crisis Rather Than\nContinuity</strong></p>\n<p>The central historical tension we now must reconcile with is\nillustrated as follows. Institutionalization provided continuity but\nfrequently sacrificed autonomy. Deinstitutionalization increased\nautonomy but often failed to create adequate continuity. This tension is\nreflected in the modern psychiatric system, specifically in the way it\nprioritizes stabilizing acute crises rather than continuity of care.</p>\n<p><strong>3.1 <em>The</em> <em>Revolving Door\nPhenomenon</em></strong></p>\n<p>One consequence of this system is the phenomenon of patients\nexperiencing frequent recurrent hospitalizations that seem to be stuck\nin an equilibrium between crisis and reintegration. This is known as the\n<em>revolving door phenomenon,</em> and is clinically defined in two\ntypes: “Type 1): at least four admissions in a 10-year period, with no\nadmission or discharge period lasting more than 2.5 years; or (Type 2):\nat least four admissions during the first 2.5 years after first\nadmission.” [3].</p>\n<p>It’s quite important to note that “This phenomenon started to occur\nafter the deinstitutionalization policies … in the second half of the\n20th century.” [3] This is because “The emphasis on\ndeinstitutionalization has increased the risk of frequent readmissions\nin many patients, now treated in the community.” [3]</p>\n<p>We don’t necessarily know exactly what causes this phenomenon, but a\n“lack of social integration may contribute to relapse.“ [3]\nAdditionally, it is speculated that “the repeated use of inpatient care,\nwhich can serve for some patients as a shelter from adverse life\nconditions, could lead to a sort of dependence on the service itself and\ncreate a vicious cycle of repetitive admissions.” [3]</p>\n<p><strong>3.2 <em>Police Involvement in Mental Crises</em></strong></p>\n<p>It’s also important to acknowledge the role law enforcement plays in\nthe system, as it can be an unavoidable part of many patients’ journeys\nwhen experiencing repeat hospitalizations. I say this because\n”contemporary health care systems, social programs, and policing models\nhave been designed in such a way that contact between people with mental\ndisorders and the police is inevitable” [6] “one in ten individuals\n[encounter] police in their pathway to mental health care,” and “25% of\npersons with mental disorders have been arrested by police at some point\nin their lifetime.” [6]</p>\n<p>Not only is police contact a recurring component of many patients’\ninteractions with navigating mental health infrastructure, but in many\ncases the consequences of such interactions can be severe, resulting in\ntragically avoidable deaths. “Overall, 203 of 633 fatal police\nencounters (32%) involved victims who showed signs of an MHC at the time\nof their death.” [7] These findings do not suggest that every police\nencounter involving mental illness is violent, but they do demonstrate\nthe risks of relying on law-enforcement systems as a routine component\nof psychiatric crisis response.</p>\n<p><strong>4. A South Asian Perspective</strong></p>\n<p>Taken together, recurrent hospitalization and police involvement\nsuggest that post-deinstitutionalization psychiatric care is not simply\ninadequate, but fragmented across hospitals, families, community\nservices, and law enforcement. Additionally, the effects of that\nfragmentation are not experienced uniformly. They are mediated by\nculture, family structure, stigma, language, and access to care, making\na South Asian lens particularly useful for examining how systemic\nlimitations are compounded in practice.</p>\n<p>Why focus on South Asians? South Asians underutilize mental-health\nservices relative to many U.S. racial/ethnic groups. Additionally,\ncultural expectations surrounding educational and occupational success,\nmarriage, family reputation, and appropriate behavior can also shape how\npsychiatric symptoms are interpreted and responded to. Mental illness\nmay therefore affect not only the individual, but also the social roles\nand expectations surrounding them, making the family an especially\nimportant part of understanding how psychiatric care is navigated.</p>\n<p><strong>4.1 The Family as a Unit</strong></p>\n<p>For many South Asian patients, the family is not simply a source of\nemotional support but an active part of psychiatric care. Family members\nmay be the first to recognize behavioral changes, facilitate entry into\ntreatment, monitor crises, make decisions, and provide support between\nepisodes of formal care. Mohsin et al. found high levels of family\ninvolvement among South Asian patients with severe mental illness, but\nthat involvement was not uniformly positive. Families ranged from highly\nsupportive to disparaging, and some relatives remained involved\nprimarily out of obligation despite strained relationships [5]. Patients\ncould therefore be heavily surrounded by family while still feeling\nmisunderstood, lonely, or inferior [5].</p>\n<p>This creates an important tension between <strong>support and\nobligation</strong>. Family involvement can provide continuity that the\nformal mental-health system often lacks, but it can also introduce\nconflict, control, or resentment. When psychiatric care is fragmented,\nfamilies may become responsible for maintaining stability between\nhospitalizations without necessarily having the knowledge, resources, or\nsupport required to do so.</p>\n<p><strong>4.2 Cultural Stigma</strong></p>\n<p>Cultural stigma complicates the role of the family because the\nconsequences of severe mental illness often extend beyond the diagnosed\nindividual. In the South Asian context, mental illness can become a\nsocial condition experienced by the entire household. Koschorke et al.\nfound that caregivers feared not only that the person with schizophrenia\nwould be judged or disrespected, but that stigma would spread outward to\nother family members through blame, avoidance, and gossip [4].\nCaregivers described anxiety that others would “spread the news,” avoid\nthe family, or interpret the presence of mental illness as evidence of\nfailure within the household itself [4].</p>\n<p>This pressure could alter how families interacted with their\ncommunities. Some caregivers restricted the affected family member’s\ncontact with others, avoided social events, or withdrew from neighbors\nand relatives because they feared uncomfortable questions or negative\nreactions [4]. In other cases, that withdrawal was imposed from the\noutside: caregivers described friends, neighbors, and acquaintances who\nstopped visiting, stopped speaking to them, or excluded them from social\nfunctions after learning about the illness [4]. The stigma attached to\nsevere mental illness therefore did not remain confined to the patient.\nCaregivers themselves could lose social standing simply because of their\nrelationship to the person who was ill.</p>\n<p>The criticism directed toward caregivers was often intensely\npersonal. Families reported being blamed for having “made” the person\nmentally ill, for failing to control their behavior, for not caring for\nthem properly, or for failing to ensure that they worked or married [4].\nOne caregiver recalled hearing others say that her husband had been\n“roaming around as mad” because she was “not taking care of him.” She\nexplained simply: “When they speak like this, I feel very bad” [4].\nAnother stated, “They criticise me. They criticise me even now for his\nbehaviour” [4]. These accounts show how responsibility for psychiatric\nillness can be displaced onto the family, even when caregivers\nthemselves are already managing the practical and emotional demands of\nsevere illness.</p>\n<p>The consequences can become particularly painful when mental illness\nthreatens expectations surrounding marriage, work, reputation, and\nfamily status. Caregivers have described being criticized for not\nmarrying an affected relative, not making sure that they worked, or even\nfor allowing a person with mental illness to enter the family through\nmarriage [4]. These reactions reflect a social environment in which\nillness is not treated solely as a health condition, but as something\ncapable of disrupting the expected social trajectory of an entire\nfamily.</p>\n<p>Some caregivers described this loss of status in devastating terms.\nOne reported that the family had effectively been labeled a “mad house”\n[4]. Another said:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“My life is over. What is there in my life now? He is not going to\nbecome normal. (…) We are not going to be respected by others.” [4]</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This statement captures how deeply stigma can reshape the caregiver’s\nown identity. The burden is not limited to managing symptoms or helping\na relative navigate treatment; it also includes the possibility of\nbecoming socially marked through association with that illness. Severe\nmental illness can therefore alter not only the patient’s social\nposition, but the perceived respectability and future of the family as a\nwhole.</p>\n<p>Perhaps most strikingly, caregivers did not always challenge this\ntreatment. Koschorke et al. note that relatively few expressed anger\ntoward people who stigmatized them. Instead, some appeared to understand\nexclusion or criticism as a natural consequence of the affected person’s\n“different behaviour” [4]. This suggests that stigma can become\ninternalized within the family itself. When social rejection is treated\nas expected or justified, families may begin adapting themselves to\nstigma rather than resisting it.</p>\n<p>This dynamic also complicates the assumption that family involvement\nis inherently protective. A family may simultaneously provide housing,\nsupervision, financial support, transportation, and crisis management\nwhile also carrying beliefs that produce shame, blame, or social\nisolation. The family can therefore function as both a source of\ncontinuity and a site where cultural stigma is reproduced. That\ncontradiction is especially important in a system that already depends\nheavily on relatives to maintain care between episodes of formal\ntreatment.</p>\n<p>The result is a deeply unstable form of informal infrastructure.\nFamilies may be expected to recognize symptoms, manage crises, encourage\ntreatment, and provide long-term support, while at the same time facing\njudgment from their communities and sometimes lacking a framework for\nunderstanding the illness themselves. In that context, stigma does more\nthan create embarrassment. It can shape whether illness is acknowledged,\nwhether care is sought, how patients are treated within the household,\nand whether caregivers themselves remain socially connected or become\nisolated.</p>\n<p>This is why cultural stigma is not peripheral to psychiatric\ninfrastructure. If the formal system relies on families to provide\ncontinuity, then the beliefs and pressures surrounding those families\nbecome part of the care system itself.</p>\n<p><strong>4.3 Lack of Education</strong></p>\n<p>Stigma is also closely connected to how mental illness is understood.\nKoschorke et al. found relatively low levels of knowledge about\nschizophrenia among caregivers, with some interpreting symptoms as\nintentional misbehavior, laziness, or a lack of effort rather than\nmanifestations of illness [4]. Other caregivers attributed symptoms to\nfamily conflict, poor upbringing, or insufficient affection [4]. These\ninterpretations can shape how families respond to emerging psychiatric\nsymptoms and may delay recognition that professional care is needed.</p>\n<p>Mohsin et al. identified a similar problem among South Asian patients\nin New York City. Many participants reported lacking the language to\ndescribe their emotional or psychiatric experiences, and some did not\nknow their formal diagnosis even after entering treatment [5]. The only\nSouth Asian term frequently identified for severe mental illness was\n‘paagal’, meaning “crazy,” which carries a strongly derogatory meaning\n[5]. Participants could often recognize that something had changed in\ntheir behavior or ability to fulfill expected family roles without\nhaving the vocabulary to identify what they were experiencing [5].</p>\n<p>Importantly, increasing biomedical knowledge alone does not\nnecessarily eliminate stigma. Koschorke et al. found that greater\nawareness of biomedical explanations for schizophrenia did not\nconsistently protect families from stigma and, in some cases, was\nassociated with greater discrimination toward patients [4]. Education\nmay therefore be most useful when it does more than simply attach a\ndiagnostic label to symptoms. Information about the nature and\nmanagement of symptoms may reduce blame, but effective interventions\nmust also account for culturally specific beliefs about illness,\nbehavior, responsibility, and family roles [4].</p>\n<p><strong>4.4 Structural and Immigration-Related Barriers</strong></p>\n<p>Cultural barriers operate alongside structural ones. Mohsin et al.\nfound that immigration status, insurance coverage, difficulty scheduling\nappointments, and fragmented relationships with multiple providers could\ninterfere with South Asian patients’ ability to access and remain\nengaged in care [5]. For first-generation immigrants in particular,\nlimited knowledge about mental health and low perceived need for\ntreatment could mean that formal psychiatric care was not accessed until\nsymptoms had already become severe [5].</p>\n<p>Immigration concerns may also intersect directly with psychiatric\ncrises. One participant described the pressure created when a parent’s\npsychiatric hospitalization occurred immediately before the family’s\ngreen-card interview, demonstrating how psychiatric instability can\ndisrupt immigration processes that depend on documentation, attendance,\nand perceived stability [5]. These pressures further complicate the\nassumption that families can simply navigate the healthcare system once\na crisis occurs.</p>\n<p>Taken together, these findings reveal a central contradiction in\npost-deinstitutionalization care: the formal system is episodic, while\nthe family is continuous. Families may provide the support that fills\nthe gaps between hospitalizations, but they may simultaneously be\nnavigating stigma, limited mental-health knowledge, strained\nrelationships, financial or immigration pressures, and fragmented\nservices. Many caregivers themselves appeared isolated and unsupported\nbecause fear of stigma led them to withdraw from social interaction\n[4].</p>\n<p><strong>5. Imagining a Better System</strong></p>\n<p>The literature reviewed thus far points to a psychiatric system that\nis fragmented across hospitals, families, community services, and law\nenforcement. For South Asian families, that fragmentation is further\ncomplicated by stigma, limited mental-health knowledge, language\nbarriers, and immigration-related constraints. If families are already\nperforming part of the work of psychiatric infrastructure, the central\nquestion becomes how formal systems can support them without simply\ntransferring responsibility for chronic psychiatric care onto them.</p>\n<p><strong>5.1 Gaps in the Literature</strong></p>\n<p>Several important gaps remain. There is limited research examining\nwhat occurs between psychiatric admissions, particularly from the\nperspective of families providing ongoing care. Research on severe\nmental illness among South Asians in the United States is also sparse;\nMohsin et al. note that there are no published prevalence or\nservice-utilization data for severe depression, bipolar disorder, and\nschizophrenia among U.S. South Asians [5]. Research is even more limited\non how South Asian families navigate recurrent severe illness over time,\nhow immigration-related pressures influence crisis response, and how\nfamilies interact with emergency or law-enforcement systems. Existing\nstudies also often capture individual encounters rather than the full\ntrajectory of repeated crises, hospitalization, discharge, and family\ncaregiving.</p>\n<p><strong>5.2 Principles for More Responsive Care</strong></p>\n<p>The literature nevertheless suggests several principles for improving\ncare. First, interventions should involve the family unit, because\nfamilies may function as both major sources of support and major sources\nof stress [5]. Second, culturally salient education should help families\nrecognize symptoms, understand available treatment, and reduce the\ntendency to interpret psychiatric symptoms as intentional behavior or\npersonal failure. However, education cannot rely on biomedical\ninformation alone; Koschorke et al. found that greater biomedical\nknowledge did not automatically reduce stigma, suggesting that\ninterventions must also address culturally specific beliefs about\nresponsibility, family reputation, and illness [4].</p>\n<p>Care must also be linguistically and culturally accessible. Language\ndifferences, dialect mismatches, lack of insurance, immigration status,\nfragmented providers, and difficulty navigating appointments can all\ninterfere with sustained treatment [5]. Earlier intervention is equally\nimportant, particularly because many South Asian patients described\nentering formal psychiatric care only after symptoms had become highly\ndisruptive or when families felt they had no other option [5].</p>\n<p><strong>5.3 Alternative Crisis-Response Infrastructure</strong></p>\n<p>Because police often become involved in mental-health crises that are\nunrelated to criminal behavior, the literature increasingly questions\nwhether law enforcement should function as the default crisis-response\nsystem [6]. Police themselves have reported frustration with gaps in\nhealth and social services that leave them responsible for situations\nthey are not well positioned to resolve [6]. Alternative response models\nthat deploy mental-health professionals instead of police for eligible\ncrisis calls, including programs such as B-HEARD and CAHOOTS, offer one\napproach to reducing unnecessary law-enforcement involvement and the\nrisk of escalation [7].</p>\n<p><strong>5.4 Designing for Care Rather Than Social\nControl</strong></p>\n<p>The literature ultimately points to a broader question about what\npsychiatric systems are designed to accomplish. Aviram argues that\npublic concern about mental-health reform often intensifies only when\nmental illness becomes socially visible or disruptive, and that\nmental-health policy has historically been shaped heavily by\nconsiderations of social control [1]. This orientation is also reflected\nin systems where treatment decisions remain largely provider-dominated\n[8], and where police and emergency services become responsible for\npeople whose needs were not adequately addressed earlier.</p>\n<p>A care-oriented system would instead prioritize continuity, patient\nparticipation, and sustained community support. Maples argues that\npatients should play an active role in directing their own care, while\nAviram emphasizes the need for a comprehensive community system\nsupported by organizational change, advocacy, and greater investment in\nmedical and social services [1], [8]. Importantly, community care should\nnot simply mean transferring unpaid responsibility from institutions to\nfamilies. Care must instead be distributed across coordinated health,\nsocial-service, family, and community systems.</p>\n<p>This also requires moving beyond episodic stabilization. People with\nsevere mental illness may need ongoing support with employment,\nbenefits, housing, treatment, and reintegration into community life [1],\n[3]. Improving outcomes therefore requires simultaneous changes in both\nhealthcare and the broader social environment, including better access,\ncommunication across agencies, patient education, and support before\ncrises become severe [8]. The goal should not simply be to contain\ncrises, but to create conditions in which fewer crises occur in the\nfirst place.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Discussion</strong></p>\n<p>Across the literature, a consistent cycle emerges. Psychiatric\ninstitutions once concentrated responsibility for people with severe\nmental illness, but deinstitutionalization redistributed that\nresponsibility across hospitals, community services, families, and\nemergency systems without always creating adequate continuity of care.\nAs a result, some patients move repeatedly through crisis,\nhospitalization, stabilization, discharge, family or community\nmanagement, deterioration, emergency or police intervention, and\nhospitalization again. South Asian families may experience this cycle\nwith additional pressures related to stigma, limited mental-health\nknowledge, cultural expectations, language barriers, immigration\nconcerns, and strained family relationships. These findings reveal\nseveral recurring tensions: community care can become burden shifting,\nfamily support can coexist with family stigma, greater knowledge does\nnot automatically eliminate stigma, and systems intended to provide care\ncan also function as mechanisms of social control.</p>\n<p>A major limitation of the existing literature is that these\nexperiences are often studied separately. Research on hospitalization\nfocuses on readmission, policing research focuses on crisis encounters,\nstigma research focuses on families, and community-care research focuses\non service delivery. Yet patients and families may experience all of\nthese stages as one continuous process. In this sense, the literature\nfragments a process that families experience continuously. Severe mental\nillness therefore cannot be understood solely at the level of the\nindividual patient; it must also be understood through the systems and\nrelationships that shape what happens before, during, and after crisis.\nUltimately, the measure of a psychiatric care system should not be\nwhether it can contain a crisis, but whether it can sustain a person\nbeyond one. At the center of that goal remains a simple principle\nidentified by participants in Mohsin et al.: “what mattered most was\nbeing treated well.” [5].</p>\n<p><strong>References</strong></p>\n<p>[1] U. Aviram, “Community care of the seriously mentally ill:\nContinuing problems and current issues,” <em>Community Mental Health\nJournal</em>, vol. 26, no. 1, pp. 69–88, Feb. 1990, doi:\n10.1007/BF00752677. <a\nhref=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2188776\"><u>PubMed</u></a></p>\n<p>[2] F. Balbuena Rivera, “From asylums to deinstitutionalization and\nafter: An analytic review,” <em>International Journal of Social\nPsychiatry</em>, vol. 70, no. 8, pp. 1392–1401, Dec. 2024, doi:\n10.1177/00207640241268356. <a\nhref=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39096009/\"><u>PubMed</u></a></p>\n<p>[3] J. Fonseca Barbosa and J. Gama Marques, “The revolving door\nphenomenon in severe psychiatric disorders: A systematic review,”\n<em>International Journal of Social Psychiatry</em>, vol. 69, no. 5, pp.\n1075–1089, Aug. 2023, doi: 10.1177/00207640221143282. <a\nhref=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37209104/\"><u>PubMed</u></a></p>\n<p>[4] M. Koschorke <em>et al</em>., “Experiences of stigma and\ndiscrimination faced by family caregivers of people with schizophrenia\nin India,” <em>Social Science &amp; Medicine</em>, vol. 178, pp. 66–77,\nApr. 2017, doi: 10.1016/j.socscimed.2017.01.061. <a\nhref=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28213300\"><u>PubMed</u></a></p>\n<p>[5] F. Mohsin, S. Aravala, T. Rahman, S. H. Ali, M. D. Taher, P.\nMitra, and S. Misra, “Psychiatric healthcare experiences of South Asian\npatients with severe mental illness diagnoses and their families in New\nYork City: A qualitative study,” <em>Community Mental Health\nJournal</em>, vol. 61, no. 1, pp. 39–49, Jan. 2025, doi:\n10.1007/s10597-024-01323-1. <a\nhref=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11703676/\"><u>PubMed\nCentral (PMC)</u></a></p>\n<p>[6] J. D. Livingston, “Contact between police and people with mental\ndisorders: A review of rates,” <em>Psychiatric Services</em>, vol. 67,\nno. 8, pp. 850–857, Aug. 2016, doi: 10.1176/appi.ps.201500312. <a\nhref=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27079990/\"><u>PubMed</u></a></p>\n<p>[7] H. Khan, M. Miller, C. Barber, and D. Azrael, “Fatal police\nshootings of victims with mental health crises: A descriptive analysis\nof data from the 2014–2015 National Violent Death Reporting System,”\n<em>Journal of Urban Health</em>, vol. 101, no. 2, pp. 262–271, Apr.\n2024, doi: 10.1007/s11524-024-00833-3. <a\nhref=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38453763/\"><u>PubMed</u></a></p>\n<p>[8] N. J. Maples, “Shared decision making in the care of post-acute\npsychiatric individuals with serious mental illness,” Dr.P.H.\ndissertation, School of Public Health, The University of Texas Health\nScience Center at Houston, Houston, TX, USA, 2020.</p>\n"}];
